 as baseline for delta predictions\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Key risk: distribution shift between training perturbations and held-out perturbations. Model must generalize at the gene level, not memorize perturbation identities.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"samples\\\": {\\n      \\\"size\\\": \\\"Total cells likely in the tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands; number of perturbations in training likely in the hundreds to low thousands. Exact counts must be read from the h5ad (n_obs, number of unique perturbation labels).\\\",\\n      \\\"distribution\\\": \\\"Cells per perturbation likely highly variable (many cells for some perturbations, few for others). Control cells may be abundant. Long-tailed distribution of cells per perturbation expected.\\\",\\n      \\\"characteristics\\\": [\\n        \\\"K562 erythroleukemia cell line\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown\\\",\\n        \\\"Pooled screen with sgRNA identity\\\",\\n        \\\"Counts per 10k + log1p normalized expression\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_variability\\\": \\\"Cell-to-cell variability in knockdown efficiency, cell cycle state, and clonal heterogeneity. Some perturbations may have strong transcriptional effects; others near-neutral.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_variability\\\": \\\"Batch effects from pooled screens, library preparation, sequencing depth, and sgRNA capture efficiency. Ambient RNA and doublets may confound perturbation assignment.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_requirements\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Recommend verifying batch/replicate columns and cell-level QC metrics before modeling.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Hold-out perturbations by gene identity (unseen-perturbation split)\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate pearson_delta of predicted vs. true change from control\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate MSE/PCC/R2 on top-20 DE genes per perturbation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting controls as baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Permutation tests / control perturbations as negative controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"PCC on top-20 DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not available from provided information; must be computed by the evaluator (Gene-Mila).\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"High pearson_delta indicates correct direction and magnitude of predicted changes. Top-20 DE gene metrics assess whether the strongest biological effects are captured.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support inputs. Recommend internal cross-validation on training perturbations (leave-perturbation-out) to estimate generalization before final submission.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation by perturbation\\\",\\n          \\\"Comparison of predicted means to control mean\\\",\\n          \\\"Sanity checks on gene-wise variance and sparsity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean as baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Random perturbation labels as negative control\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. observed mean expression\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"Delta correlation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not available; to be assessed during model development.\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical validation ensures predictions are not dominated by normalization artifacts or control mean leakage.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Recommend checking that predictions are not simply the control mean (which would yield zero delta correlation).\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"data_properties\\\": {\\n    \\\"distribution\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Log1p-normalized counts per 10k; expected overdispersion typical of scRNA-seq\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene expression dominated by highly expressed genes (e.g., ribosomal, mitochondrial)\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects may be subtle for most genes and strong for a few\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Biological variability across cells within a perturbation due to cell cycle, clonal heterogeneity, and variable knockdown efficiency.\\\",\\n        \\\"outliers\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cells with unusually high mitochondrial or ribosomal content\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublets or cells with mixed sgRNA assignment\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Expression distributions should be inspected per perturbation and per gene; heavy tails and zero-inflation are expected.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Strong effects expected for essential genes and known regulators; weak effects for unrelated genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_implications\\\": \\\"No decision support. Model should account for overdispersion and sparsity; mean prediction is the target, so cell-level noise should average out.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library size variation across cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-specific shifts in gene detection\\\",\\n          \\\"sgRNA capture efficiency variation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Technical variability from sequencing depth, batch, and cell quality.\\\",\\n        \\\"artifacts\\\": [\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA contamination\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-quality cells with high mitochondrial fraction\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical artifacts can bias perturbation means if not controlled.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cell-level QC filtering\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction or inclusion of batch covariates\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet detection and removal\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend inspecting obs columns for QC metrics (n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito) and batch labels.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"features\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"mRNA gene expression\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation target gene identity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"All genes are potential readouts; top-20 DE genes per perturbation are most relevant for scoring.\\\",\\n        \\\"selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for mean prediction (required output dimension)\\\",\\n          \\\"For modeling, consider highly variable genes or genes with detectable expression\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Gene-level mean expression is the prediction target; feature selection is for model efficiency, not output reduction.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that selected features capture known perturbation effects (e.g., knockdown of target gene itself).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend retaining all genes in the output and using HVGs or all genes for model training depending on method.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"Gene identifiers (same order as input)\\\",\\n          \\\"Normalization factors (counts per 10k)\\\",\\n          \\\"sgRNA assignments\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Gene annotation quality assumed consistent; normalization already applied.\\\",\\n        \\\"selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter genes with zero expression across all cells (if any) for modeling, but output must include all genes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical features should not drive biological predictions.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify gene order and normalization match the evaluator's expectations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical\\\": \\\"No decision support. Ensure predictions.npz has perts in the exact order of heldout_perturbations.json and means with shape (n_perts, n_genes).\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Number of cells per perturbation\\\",\\n          \\\" Knockdown efficiency (target gene expression reduction)\\\",\\n          \\\"Reproducibility of perturbation effects across cells\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Quality varies by perturbation; some may have few cells and noisy means.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low cell numbers for some perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n          \\\"Off-target effects\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Weighted mean estimation by cell quality\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrinkage of perturbation means toward control for low-cell perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare known essential gene knockdowns to expected phenotypes.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend computing per-perturbation cell counts and target gene knockdown to assess training data quality.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"n_genes per cell\\\",\\n          \\\"total counts per cell\\\",\\n          \\\"percent mitochondrial reads\\\",\\n          \\\"doublet scores\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Standard scRNA-seq QC metrics should be computed from the h5ad.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low-quality cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high pct_mito\\\",\\n          \\\"Remove doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch as covariate or correct\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means for training.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"noise\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cell cycle heterogeneity\\\",\\n          \\\"Clonal variation\\\",\\n          \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n          \\\"Stochastic gene expression\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Increases variance of perturbation means, especially for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Estimate by comparing within-perturbation variance to between-perturbation variance.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use robust mean estimation (e.g., trimmed mean)\\\",\\n          \\\"Model cell-level covariates (cell cycle) if available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_noise\\\": \\\"No decision support. Biological noise is irreducible but can be averaged out with sufficient cells.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library size variation\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA\\\",\\n          \\\"Sequencing depth\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Can bias perturbation means if correlated with perturbation assignment.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Normalization (already applied as counts per 10k + log1p)\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction or covariate adjustment\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA correction (e.g., SoupX) if raw counts available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare means before and after batch correction.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\": \\\"No decision support. Since normalization is already applied, focus on batch effects and cell quality.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalization\\\": {\\n      \\\"strategies\\\": {\\n        \\\"recommendations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use the provided counts per 10k + log1p normalization as-is for both training and prediction output\\\",\\n          \\\"Do not re-normalize if it would break compatibility with the evaluator\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The evaluator expects predictions in the same normalized space as the input; altering normalization would invalidate scoring.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad, use X as normalized expression. For modeling, optionally standardize features per gene (z-score) but inverse-transform predictions to the original space.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_normalization\\\": \\\"No decision support. Strictly preserve the provided normalization for output.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality_control\\\": {\\n        \\\"procedures\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high mitochondrial fraction\\\",\\n          \\\"Remove doublets if scores available\\\",\\n          \\\"Filter perturbations with too few cells for stable mean estimation (but keep for training if possible)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"n_genes > 200 (adjust based on distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"pct_mito < 20% (adjust based on distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet score threshold\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Compute QC metrics from raw counts if available; otherwise from normalized X (less ideal). Use scanpy.pp.calculate_qc_metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that QC does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure remaining cells retain expected perturbation effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_qc\\\": \\\"No decision support. QC thresholds should be data-driven (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD).\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feature_selection\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output\\\",\\n          \\\"For modeling, consider highly variable genes (HVGs) or genes with nonzero expression in controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"HVG top 2000-5000 for model input\\\",\\n          \\\"Exclude genes with zero variance in controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes on control cells or all cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that HVGs include known perturbation-responsive genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in feature space (they must be, since output requires all genes).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"No decision support. Feature selection is optional for model efficiency; output must cover all genes.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"batch_correction\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Include batch as covariate in model\\\",\\n          \\\"ComBat or Harmony if batch labels available and effects strong\\\",\\n          \\\"Regress out technical covariates (e.g., cell cycle) if appropriate\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Significant association between batch and perturbation assignment\\\",\\n          \\\"Visual inspection of batch effects (e.g., PCA)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Check obs columns for batch/replicate. Use scanpy or scvi-tools for integration if needed.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after correction; ensure biological effects preserved.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Known perturbation effects should remain consistent across batches.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_batch\\\": \\\"No decision support. Batch correction is risky if batch is confounded with perturbation; verify design first.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Leave-perturbation-out cross-validation\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate pearson_delta on held-out training perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Check top-20 DE gene recovery\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta > 0.3 (expected for good models)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC > 0.5\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Split training perturbations into folds; train on all but one fold; evaluate on held-out fold using same metrics as evaluator.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Ensure cross-validation split mimics unseen-perturbation setting (no cell leakage).\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted effects for known regulators are biologically plausible.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support. Cross-validation is essential given no direct validation set.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Check prediction shape and order\\\",\\n          \\\"Verify no NaN/Inf in predictions\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare prediction distribution to training mean distribution\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"means shape == (n_perts, n_genes)\\\",\\n          \\\"perts order matches heldout_perturbations.json\\\",\\n          \\\"Predictions within reasonable range of training expression\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load predictions.npz and assert shapes and order.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run a dummy submission with control mean to ensure evaluator accepts format.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure float dtype and no compression issues.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support. Format compliance is critical.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"quality_assessment\\\": {\\n    \\\"completeness\\\": {\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"Training file contains many perturbations but held-out perturbations have zero cells. Coverage of gene space is complete (all genes required in output).\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_data\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbations entirely missing from training (by design). Some genes may be zero-inflated or undetected in some perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Completeness is adequate for training but generalization to unseen perturbations is the key challenge.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use gene-level models that generalize across perturbations (e.g., linear models on gene features, baseline control mean plus predicted delta)\\\",\\n        \\\"Leverage similarity between perturbations (e.g., pathway/network information) if available\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_completeness\\\": \\\"No decision support. The unseen-perturbation setting requires models that do not rely on perturbation identity.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": \\\"K562 CRISPRi screen is a standard perturbation dataset; gene expression readout is biologically relevant.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical\\\": \\\"Normalization and gene space are relevant to the evaluator's metrics.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Data is relevant for the task; main risk is distribution shift to unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that training perturbations cover diverse gene functions.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Known biology of K562 (erythroleukemia) should inform expectations.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_relevance\\\": \\\"No decision support. Relevance is high given matched cell line and perturbation type.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n      \\\"technical\\\": \\\"Normalization is fixed; reproducibility depends on model training and random seeds.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological\\\": \\\"Biological reproducibility across cells within a perturbation can be estimated from training data.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Technical reproducibility should be high if pipeline is deterministic; biological reproducibility varies.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run model with multiple seeds and\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"problem_definition\\\": {\\n    \\\"formal_definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological_context\\\": \\\"We are predicting the transcriptome-wide mean expression response of K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells to CRISPRi knockdown of individual genes (perturbations) that were never observed during training. The training data consist of single-cell RNA-seq profiles from K562 cells under many CRISPRi perturbations and controls. The held-out perturbations are unseen genes; the model must generalize from gene-level features and perturbation structure to unseen knockdowns, producing the mean log1p CP10K expression for all genes in the same order and normalization as the input.\\\",\\n      \\\"input_output_mapping\\\": \\\"Input: training scRNA-seq matrix X (cells x genes) with perturbation labels and control cells; held-out perturbation list P_held (n_perts); gene-level metadata (gene identifiers, optionally embeddings/annotations). Output: means matrix M (n_perts x n_genes) where M[i,j] is the predicted mean log1p CP10K expression of gene j in K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbation P_held[i]. Evaluation compares M to held-out true perturbation means, focusing on delta from control (pearson_delta), overall MSE, and top-20 DE gene MSE/PCC/R2.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_constraints\\\": [\\n        \\\"Predictions must be non-negative after inverse log1p transform and consistent with CP10K normalization.\\\",\\n        \\\"Predicted means should reflect biologically plausible expression ranges for K562 genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation effects should be sparse and concentrated on biologically related genes/pathways.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control baseline must be estimated from training control cells, not assumed zero.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are gene-specific and often weak, requiring shrinkage toward control.\\\",\\n        \\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no direct training examples; model must rely on gene-gene relationships and functional similarity.\\\",\\n        \\\"No external data from the same screen may be used; only cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"evaluation_criteria\\\": [\\n        \\\"Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control per perturbation (pearson_delta).\\\",\\n        \\\"Mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and true means across all genes and perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 restricted to the top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Biological plausibility: predicted effects should align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi knockdown direction.\\\",\\n        \\\"Reproducibility: predictions must be generated from a deterministic or seeded pipeline using only provided data.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"success_metrics\\\": [\\n        \\\"pearson_delta above a baseline of zero-effect (control-only) prediction and above simple linear baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE lower than control-only baseline and lower than naive gene-mean baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC and R2 significantly above zero-effect baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"Consistent improvement across perturbations rather than driven by a few outliers.\\\",\\n        \\\"Biological validation: predicted top genes for known pathways show expected knockdown signatures where prior knowledge exists.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"key_variables\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": [\\n        \\\"Perturbation target gene identity and its functional role in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"Baseline expression of target gene and pathway members.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships and co-expression modules.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown efficiency and off-target effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Cell cycle, stress, and culture-state variation in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"Copy-number alterations and karyotype of K562 affecting expression.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"technical\\\": [\\n        \\\"Single-cell library size and dropout.\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch effects across perturbation plates or runs.\\\",\\n        \\\"Guide RNA representation and multiplicity of infection.\\\",\\n        \\\"Normalization (CP10K, log1p) and gene filtering.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbation list and gene ordering.\\\",\\n        \\\"Train/test split by perturbation (unseen-perturbation setting).\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"relationships\\\": [\\n        \\\"Perturbation target gene expression is reduced in its own knockdown cells (on-target effect).\\\",\\n        \\\"Knockdown of a regulator propagates to downstream targets and pathways.\\\",\\n        \\\"Genes with similar functions or co-expression tend to have correlated perturbation responses.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control mean expression is the baseline from which deltas are computed.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations may share pathway membership with training perturbations, enabling transfer.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"constraints\\\": [\\n        \\\"Only cellforge_input.h5ad may be used for training.\\\",\\n        \\\"No held-out perturbation cells are available for training or fine-tuning.\\\",\\n        \\\"Output must match gene order and normalization exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"Predictions are means, not single-cell distributions.\\\",\\n        \\\"Computational resources should be reasonable for a single-machine pipeline.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation_requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Hold out a subset of training perturbations to simulate unseen-perturbation prediction.\\\",\\n        \\\"Compare against control-only and gene-mean baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"Report pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics on internal validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Check biological coherence of predicted top genes for validation perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Ensure deterministic seeds and documented preprocessing.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"scope\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological_scope\\\": \\\"K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbations; prediction of mean transcriptional response to unseen gene knockdowns; focus on gene-level mean expression and delta from control.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_scope\\\": \\\"Single-cell RNA-seq matrix processing, control baseline estimation, feature construction from gene identity and expression, regression/transfer learning from training perturbations to held-out perturbations, and evaluation with Gene-Mila metrics.\\\",\\n      \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n        \\\"Cannot model single-cell heterogeneity or cell-state-specific responses.\\\",\\n        \\\"Limited ability to predict strong effects for perturbations with no pathway overlap with training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi efficiency and off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"K562-specific copy-number and culture effects may confound generalization.\\\",\\n        \\\"Evaluation is on means, so sparse or bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Control cells in training are representative of the control baseline for held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene identifiers are consistent and sufficient to derive functional features.\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function and pathway membership.\\\",\\n        \\\"Mean expression is a meaningful summary of perturbation response.\\\",\\n        \\\"Training perturbations cover enough functional diversity to allow transfer to held-out perturbations.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use internal held-out perturbations to verify that predicted deltas recover known pathway signatures, that on-target knockdown is reflected for target genes when possible, and that top predicted genes are enriched for relevant biological processes.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"key_challenges\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no close functional analog in training.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are often weak and variable, making deltas hard to predict.\\\",\\n        \\\"K562 has complex copy-number alterations that distort expression and perturbation response.\\\",\\n        \\\"Pathway redundancy and feedback can mask or reverse expected effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation effects may depend on cell state not captured in mean expression.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Poor generalization to functionally isolated perturbations and underestimation of true effects, reducing pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use gene functional embeddings and pathway annotations to relate held-out targets to training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Shrink predictions toward control to avoid spurious large effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Model perturbation effects as low-rank or pathway-level modules rather than independent gene effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Incorporate target gene baseline expression and network centrality as features.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use robust control baseline estimation across batches.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Internal leave-perturbation-out validation stratified by pathway and target expression to assess generalization across functional categories.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_considerations\\\": \\\"Predictions should respect directionality of known regulatory relationships and avoid biologically implausible large effects for genes not connected to the target.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"High-dimensional output (thousands of genes) with limited number of perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Sparse single-cell data and dropout affecting mean estimates.\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch effects between perturbation groups and controls.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision.\\\",\\n        \\\"Need to match exact gene order and normalization.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Overfitting to training perturbations, noisy control baseline, and poor calibration of predicted means.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Aggregate to pseudo-bulk means per perturbation to reduce noise.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use low-rank regression, factor models, or kernel methods on gene features.\\\",\\n        \\\"Apply strong regularization and cross-validation over perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Estimate control mean robustly using all control cells and batch correction.\\\",\\n        \\\"Validate output shape, gene order, and normalization against input.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation by perturbation with held-out metric computation and sanity checks on output dimensions and value ranges.\\\",\\n      \\\"implementation_requirements\\\": \\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and optionally torch; deterministic seeds; memory sufficient for the full matrix and model.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"data_quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"issues\\\": [\\n        \\\"Variable cell numbers per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Low-expression genes with high dropout.\\\",\\n        \\\"Potential guide-level or batch confounding.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control cells may differ in quality from perturbed cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"Possible doublets or low-quality cells.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Noisy perturbation means and biased control baseline, leading to inaccurate delta predictions.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Filter low-quality cells and genes with very low detection.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use pseudo-bulk averaging and variance stabilization.\\\",\\n        \\\"Include batch/plate covariates if available.\\\",\\n        \\\"Robustly estimate control mean with trimming or median-based approaches.\\\",\\n        \\\"Downsample or weight perturbations to avoid domination by large groups.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure filtered genes retain key K562 markers and that control baseline matches known expression profiles.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"computational\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"Large gene dimension makes full covariance modeling expensive.\\\",\\n        \\\"Need efficient cross-validation over many perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Balancing model complexity with limited perturbation samples.\\\",\\n        \\\"Reproducible pipeline for held-out prediction.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Long runtimes and risk of overfitting or unstable predictions.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use low-rank or factorized models (e.g., PCA on gene space, reduced-rank regression).\\\",\\n        \\\"Precompute gene features and control mean once.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use efficient linear algebra and sparse matrices where possible.\\\",\\n        \\\"Limit hyperparameter search with nested cross-validation.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Measure runtime and memory, and verify results are stable across seeds.\\\",\\n      \\\"resource_requirements\\\": \\\"Single workstation with at least 32 GB RAM and multi-core CPU; optional GPU for deep models but not required.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n      \\\"requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Predictions should be attributable to target gene function and pathway membership.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top predicted genes should be biologically coherent.\\\",\\n        \\\"Model should allow inspection of learned gene-gene relationships.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"High-dimensional regression coefficients are hard to interpret.\\\",\\n        \\\"Latent factor models may obscure biological meaning.\\\",\\n        \\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"solutions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use pathway-level or module-level parameters that map to biological processes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Report top predicted genes with pathway enrichment.\\\",\\n        \\\"Compare predicted signatures to known perturbation signatures where available.\\\",\\n        \\\"Provide feature importance at gene and pathway level.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check enrichment of predicted top genes for pathways related to the target gene and consistency with known K562 biology.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Expert review of predicted signatures for selected held-out perturbations against literature or pathway databases.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"research_questions\\\": {\\n    \\\"primary\\\": {\\n      \\\"questions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Can a model trained on seen CRISPRi perturbations in K562 predict mean transcriptional responses to unseen perturbations using only gene identity and expression features?\\\",\\n        \\\"Which feature representation (gene expression, functional embeddings, pathway membership) best enables transfer to unseen perturbations?\\\",\\n        \\\"How much shrinkage toward control is optimal for maximizing pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics?\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"hypotheses\\\": [\\n        \\\"Perturbation responses are partially predictable from target gene function and co-expression, enabling above-baseline pearson_delta for held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Low-rank pathway-level models outperform independent gene-wise regression for unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Shrinkage toward control improves MSE and top-20 DE metrics by reducing spurious effects.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Demonstrates whether functional similarity and network structure can substitute for direct perturbation data, informing perturbation prediction in cancer cell lines.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_approach\\\": \\\"Leave-perturbation-out cross-validation within training data, stratified by pathway and target expression, with metric comparison to baselines.\\\",\\n      \\\"expected_outcomes\\\": [\\n        \\\"Positive pearson_delta for a substantial fraction of held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Improved top-20 DE metrics relative to control-only baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"Pathway-level models showing better generalization than gene-wise models.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"secondary\\\": {\\n      \\\"questions\\\": [\\n        \\\"How do CRISPRi on-target effects manifest in predicted means for target genes?\\\",\\n        \\\"Which gene modules are most predictable across perturbations?\\\",\\n        \\\"How does target gene baseline expression affect prediction accuracy?\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"hypotheses\\\": [\\n        \\\"Target genes with higher baseline expression show stronger and more predictable knockdown effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Co-expression modules with strong regulatory structure are more predictable than isolated genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Predictability varies by pathway and target essentiality.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Reveals which parts of the transcriptome are robustly predictable and which require direct measurement.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_approach\\\": \\\"Analyze prediction errors by gene module, target expression, and pathway annotation.\\\",\\n      \\\"expected_outcomes\\\": [\\n        \\\"Higher accuracy for highly expressed and network-central genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Identification of predictable versus unpredictable modules.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"biological_mechanisms\\\": {\\n      \\\"mechanisms\\\": [\\n        \\\"CRISPRi-mediated transcriptional repression of target gene.\\\",\\n        \\\"Downstream propagation through regulatory networks.\\\",\\n        \\\"Compensatory feedback and pathway redundancy.\\\",\\n        \\\"Cell-state-dependent modulation of perturbation response.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"investigation_approach\\\": \\\"Model perturbation effects as combinations of target-gene repression and pathway-level propagation, using gene co-expression and functional similarity.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Compare predicted target-gene knockdown direction with expectation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Pathway enrichment of predicted top genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Comparison with known perturbation signatures from literature where available.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"expected_insights\\\": [\\n        \\\"Identification of pathway-level response modules.\\\",\\n        \\\"Quantification of predictability by regulatory distance from target.\\\",\\n        \\\"Insights into K562-specific network architecture.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use pathway databases and literature to confirm that predicted responses for selected perturbations align with known biology.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"experimental_validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations with true means for evaluation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control cells matched to perturbation conditions.\\\",\\n        \\\"Consistent normalization and gene filtering.\\\",\\n        \\\"Sufficient cell numbers for stable mean estimation.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Perturb-seq or CRISPRi scRNA-seq in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"Pseudo-bulk mean estimation per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Differential expression analysis for top-20 genes.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"controls\\\": [\\n        \\\"Non-targeting control guides.\\\",\\n        \\\"Safe-harbor controls.\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch-matched controls.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n        \\\"pearson_delta per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE across genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, R2.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm predicted effects for selected perturbations with independent qPCR or targeted RNA-seq where feasible.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"analysis_methods\\\": {\\n    \\\"computational_approaches\\\": {\\n      \\\"methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Pseudo-bulk mean estimation per perturbation and control.\\\",\\n        \\\"Delta-from-control regression with low-rank gene factors (PCA/truncated SVD).\\\",\\n        \\\"Kernel regression or similarity-weighted averaging over training perturbations using gene functional embeddings.\\\",\\n        \\\"Reduced-rank regression mapping perturbation features to gene expression deltas.\\\",\\n        \\\"Optional neural network with gene embeddings and perturbation embeddings, trained with strong regularization.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction requires transferring information from training perturbations via gene-level features and low-dimensional structure, while controlling overfitting.\\\",\\n      \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Preprocess anndata, compute control mean, build perturbation-by-gene delta matrix, compute gene features (expression, co-expression, optional pathway embeddings), train models with leave-perturbation-out cross-validation, select best model, predict held-out means, and save npz.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation by perturbation with metric computation and comparison to baselines; sanity checks on output.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Enrichment analysis of predicted top genes for validation perturbations and consistency with known K562 biology.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation_strategies\\\": {\\n      \\\"strategies\\\": [\\n        \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Stratified cross-validation by pathway and target expression.\\\",\\n        \\\"Baseline comparison: control-only, gene-mean, and linear regression on target expression.\\\",\\n        \\\"Ensemble of models to assess stability.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Simulates the unseen-perturbation setting and quantifies generalization.\\\",\\n      \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Split training perturbations into folds, train on folds, predict held-out fold, compute metrics, and aggregate.\\\",\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n        \\\"pearson_delta\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE\\\",\\n        \\\"top-20 DE MSE, PCC, R2\\\",\\n        \\\"Spearman correlation of deltas\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that validation predictions recover pathway signatures and on-target knockdown where applicable.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"analysis_pipelines\\\": {\\n      \\\"pipelines\\\": [\\n        \\\"Data loading and QC: load h5ad, filter low-quality cells/genes, extract perturbation labels and control cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"Pseudo-bulk aggregation: compute mean log1p CP10K per perturbation and control.\\\",\\n        \\\"Feature construction: gene expression features, co-expression modules, optional functional embeddings.\\\",\\n        \\\"Model training: low-rank regression or kernel regression with cross-validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Prediction: generate means for held-out perturbations and inverse-transform if needed.\\\",\\n        \\\"Evaluation: compute metrics on internal validation and format output.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"components\\\": [\\n        \\\"anndata/scanpy for data handling\\\",\\n        \\\"numpy/scipy for linear algebra\\\",\\n        \\\"scikit-learn for regression and cross-validation\\\",\\n        \\\"optional torch for neural models\\\",\\n        \\\"custom scripts for metric computation and npz output\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"workflow\\\": \\\"QC -> pseudo-bulk -> features -> cross-validated model selection -> final training on all training perturbations -> prediction on held-out -> output npz.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Unit tests for output shape and normalization; cross-validation metrics logged; comparison to baselines.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_interpretation\\\": \\\"Map predicted deltas to pathways and modules; report top genes per perturbation with enrichment.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"experimental_validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Independent CRISPRi scRNA\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"baseline_models\": {\n    \"literature_review\": {\n      \"existing_methods\": {\n        \"methods\": [\n          \"Linear baseline (Ridge regression from perturbation one-hot to mean expression)\",\n          \"Mean/control baseline (predict control mean for all perturbations)\",\n          \"Latent linear models (PCA + regression, e.g., scGen-style linear approximation)\",\n          \"Autoencoder-based perturbation prediction (e.g., scGen, CPA)\",\n          \"Graph neural network models leveraging gene regulatory networks (e.g., GEARS)\",\n          \"Foundation-model zero-shot baselines (e.g., scGPT, Geneformer fine-tuned or prompted)\",\n          \"Simple additive/effect-size transfer models (e.g., Sci-Plex-style linear effect transfer)\",\n          \"Nearest-neighbor / similarity-based perturbation transfer (e.g., using perturbation embeddings)\"\n        ],\n        \"strengths\": [\n          \"Linear baselines are highly interpretable, fast, and robust in low-data regimes; they often perform competitively for mean-expression prediction.\",\n          \"Control-mean baseline provides a lower bound and reveals how much signal is above biological/technical noise.\",\n          \"Autoencoder and latent-variable models can capture nonlinear expression manifolds and disentangle perturbation effects.\",\n          \"Graph-based models (GEARS) incorporate biological prior knowledge (gene-gene relationships) and can generalize to unseen perturbations via pathway similarity.\",\n          \"Foundation models leverage large-scale pretraining and may capture general gene-regulatory patterns, enabling zero-shot or few-shot prediction.\",\n          \"Effect-size transfer models are simple and biologically motivated: they assume perturbations act as additive shifts in expression space.\"\n        ],\n        \"limitations\": [\n          \"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects; they may underfit complex regulatory responses.\",\n          \"Control-mean baseline ignores perturbation-specific effects entirely and is only useful as a sanity check.\",\n          \"Autoencoder-based methods often require large training sets of perturbations and may overfit to seen perturbations; generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging.\",\n          \"Graph neural networks depend heavily on the quality and completeness of prior networks; K562-specific regulatory networks may be incomplete.\",\n          \"Foundation models are computationally expensive, may have data leakage concerns, and often require fine-tuning to achieve competitive performance on specific screens.\",\n          \"Most methods predict single-cell distributions or pseudobulk, but the task requires mean expression; model outputs may need careful aggregation.\",\n          \"Evaluation metrics (e.g., Pearson delta) can be dominated by highly expressed genes, and top-20 DE gene metrics are sensitive to noise.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"Perturbation prediction models should capture biologically meaningful gene programs (e.g., CRISPRi knockdown effects on essential genes, pathway responses). Simple linear/additive models reflect the assumption that perturbation effects are approximately additive in log-normalized space, which is a reasonable first-order approximation. Graph-based and foundation models aim to encode regulatory relationships, but their biological interpretability is often limited. For K562 CRISPRi, the most relevant signals are likely direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses; models that explicitly model gene-gene interactions or use pathway priors may be more biologically grounded.\"\n      },\n      \"model_comparison\": {\n        \"comparison_criteria\": [\n          \"Predictive accuracy on unseen perturbations (Pearson delta, MSE, top-20 DE metrics)\",\n          \"Generalization ability to held-out perturbations and cell types\",\n          \"Biological interpretability of learned representations and predictions\",\n          \"Computational efficiency (training time, inference time, memory)\",\n          \"Data efficiency (performance as a function of training set size)\",\n          \"Robustness to biological and technical noise (batch effects, stochasticity)\",\n          \"Ease of implementation and reproducibility\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta (change from control) per perturbation\",\n          \"Mean squared error (MSE) on mean expression\",\n          \"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 on top-20 differentially expressed genes\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene rankings\",\n          \"Area under the precision-recall curve for identifying differentially expressed genes\",\n          \"Cosine similarity between predicted and true perturbation effect vectors\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": \"Linear models offer direct interpretability via coefficients that can be mapped to gene effects. Graph-based models can highlight pathways and gene modules. Foundation models are generally black-box, though attention weights may provide some insight. For biological validation, predicted top DE genes should be compared to known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes leads to growth arrest signatures).\",\n        \"computational_requirements\": \"Linear baselines require minimal compute (seconds to minutes on CPU). Autoencoder and graph models require GPUs and hours of training. Foundation models require significant GPU memory and may need fine-tuning, which can take days. For this task, given the dataset size and evaluation constraints, simple baselines are likely sufficient and more practical.\"\n      },\n      \"recommendations\": {\n        \"model_selection\": [\n          \"Start with strong simple baselines: control-mean, linear regression (Ridge) from perturbation embeddings (e.g., one-hot or learned embeddings) to mean expression, and effect-size transfer.\",\n          \"Implement a latent linear model (PCA on control cells + regression) to capture major expression axes.\",\n          \"If resources permit, evaluate a graph-based model (GEARS) or a lightweight autoencoder (scGen) for comparison.\",\n          \"Avoid heavy foundation models unless zero-shot performance is competitive and computational budget allows.\"\n        ],\n        \"implementation_priority\": [\n          \"First, establish control-mean and linear regression baselines to set a performance floor.\",\n          \"Second, implement effect-size transfer and PCA-based regression.\",\n          \"Third, if time permits, implement a simple neural network (MLP) on perturbation embeddings.\",\n          \"Focus on robust cross-validation and evaluation using the specified metrics.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Validate predictions by checking whether known K562 CRISPRi effects are recovered (e.g., knockdown of ribosomal genes leads to downregulation of translation machinery). Compare predicted top DE genes with known pathways. Use gene set enrichment analysis on predicted effect vectors to assess biological plausibility.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"evaluation_framework\": {\n      \"metrics\": {\n        \"primary_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control\",\n          \"MSE on mean expression across all genes\",\n          \"MSE on top-20 DE genes (per perturbation, averaged)\"\n        ],\n        \"secondary_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson correlation on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"R2 on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\",\n          \"Cosine similarity between predicted and true delta vectors\",\n          \"Mean absolute error (MAE)\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_metrics\": [\n          \"Recovery of known pathway signatures (e.g., GSEA on predicted deltas)\",\n          \"Precision-recall for identifying true DE genes\",\n          \"Correlation with known essential gene knockdown signatures\",\n          \"Conservation of gene-gene co-expression patterns in predicted profiles\"\n        ],\n        \"validation_metrics\": [\n          \"Cross-validation performance across held-out perturbations\",\n          \"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits\",\n          \"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\",\n          \"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"validation_strategy\": {\n        \"cross_validation\": \"Use leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation on the training set to estimate generalization to unseen perturbations. Given the held-out set is fixed, we can simulate the unseen-perturbation setting by holding out perturbations from training. Stratify by perturbation strength or pathway to ensure representative splits.\",\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Assess biological plausibility of predictions via pathway enrichment and comparison to known K562 CRISPRi phenotypes. Use independent data (if available) or literature-based signatures to validate top predicted effects.\",\n        \"independent_testing\": \"The provided held-out perturbations serve as an independent test set. Ensure no data leakage: models must not see any cells from held-out perturbations during training. Use only the provided input file for training.\",\n        \"robustness_testing\": \"Evaluate model performance under different normalization schemes, random gene subsampling, and noise addition. Test sensitivity to hyperparameters and random initialization.\"\n      },\n      \"benchmarks\": {\n        \"baseline_models\": [\n          \"Control-mean predictor (predicts control mean for all perturbations)\",\n          \"Linear regression (Ridge) with one-hot perturbation encoding\",\n          \"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen)\",\n          \"PCA-based linear model (PCA on control, regression on perturbation)\"\n        ],\n        \"state_of_the_art\": [\n          \"GEARS (graph-enhanced perturbation prediction)\",\n          \"scGen (autoencoder-based)\",\n          \"CPA (compositional perturbation autoencoder)\",\n          \"Foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) if applicable\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_benchmarks\": [\n          \"Known K562 CRISPRi effects from literature (e.g., knockdown of essential genes)\",\n          \"Pathway-level concordance with known perturbation signatures\",\n          \"Gene-gene correlation structure preservation\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_targets\": [\n          \"Pearson delta > 0.3 (indicative of meaningful signal)\",\n          \"Top-20 DE MSE lower than control-mean baseline\",\n          \"R2 on top-20 DE genes > 0.1\",\n          \"Performance significantly better than control-mean baseline (p < 0.05 via permutation test)\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"performance_analysis\": {\n      \"model_performance\": {\n        \"accuracy_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson delta per perturbation (mean and distribution)\",\n          \"MSE across all genes\",\n          \"Top-20 DE MSE, Pearson, R2\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene effects\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"Models that capture direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses will show higher Pearson delta. Simple linear models may perform well if perturbation effects are additive in log space. Biological relevance can be assessed by enrichment of predicted top genes in known K562 pathways.\",\n        \"generalization\": \"Generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging. Linear models may generalize if perturbations share additive effects. Graph-based models may generalize better by leveraging gene network similarity. Performance should be evaluated via leave-one-perturbation-out CV.\",\n        \"robustness\": \"Models should be robust to technical noise (dropout, batch effects) and biological variability. Regularized linear models are typically robust. Neural networks may overfit and require careful regularization.\"\n      },\n      \"comparative_analysis\": {\n        \"baseline_comparison\": \"Control-mean baseline will have near-zero Pearson delta and high MSE. Linear regression and effect-size transfer are expected to outperform it. The gap between linear and more complex models indicates the extent of nonlinearity in perturbation effects.\",\n        \"state_of_the_art_comparison\": \"State-of-the-art models like GEARS may outperform linear baselines on unseen perturbations if gene network priors are informative. However, given the small size of many perturbation screens, simple models often remain competitive. Foundation models may be strong zero-shot but require significant resources.\",\n        \"biological_comparison\": \"Predictions should be compared at the pathway level: models that recover known K562 CRISPRi signatures (e.g., ribosome biogenesis downregulation upon knockdown of ribosomal genes) are more biologically valid.\",\n        \"computational_comparison\": \"Linear models are orders of magnitude faster and require less memory. The performance gain of complex models must justify the computational cost.\"\n      },\n      \"strengths_weaknesses\": {\n        \"strengths\": [\n          \"Simple linear models are interpretable, fast, and robust.\",\n          \"Effect-size transfer leverages seen perturbations and can generalize to similar perturbations.\",\n          \"Graph-based models incorporate biological priors and may capture pathway-level effects.\",\n          \"Ensemble approaches can combine strengths of multiple models.\"\n        ],\n        \"weaknesses\": [\n          \"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial effects.\",\n          \"Complex models may overfit to seen perturbations and fail on unseen ones.\",\n          \"Evaluation metrics may be dominated by highly expressed genes; top-20 DE metrics are noisy.\",\n          \"Limited training data for unseen perturbations makes generalization difficult.\",\n          \"Biological interpretability of deep models is limited.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_insights\": [\n          \"CRISPRi perturbations often produce subtle expression changes; effect sizes may be small.\",\n          \"Essential gene knockdowns may induce stress responses that dominate expression changes.\",\n          \"Gene-gene correlations in control cells provide a baseline for expected co-expression.\",\n          \"Pathway-level analysis can reveal whether models capture coordinated responses.\"\n        ],\n        \"technical_limitations\": [\n          \"Single-cell data sparsity and dropout can obscure true mean expression.\",\n          \"Normalization and log transformation may distort additive effects.\",\n          \"Batch effects and technical variability can confound predictions.\",\n          \"Limited number of perturbations in training set restricts model complexity.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"improvement_suggestions\": {\n      \"model_enhancements\": {\n        \"architectural_improvements\": [\n          \"Incorporate gene-gene interaction networks (e.g., from STRING or K562-specific networks) into models.\",\n          \"Use attention mechanisms to weight genes by relevance to perturbation.\",\n          \"Develop hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections.\",\n          \"Explore multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength.\"\n        ],\n        \"training_optimizations\": [\n          \"Use regularization (L2, dropout) to prevent overfitting on small perturbation sets.\",\n          \"Employ data augmentation (e.g., noise injection, gene dropout) to improve robustness.\",\n          \"Use cross-validation for hyperparameter tuning.\",\n          \"Consider transfer learning from larger perturbation screens (if allowed, but task restricts to provided data).\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": [\n          \"Incorporate pathway annotations as features or constraints.\",\n          \"Use sparse models to identify key genes driving predictions.\",\n          \"Post-hoc interpretability via gene set enrichment on model weights or predictions.\",\n       