sion across perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_normalization\\\": \\\"No decision support. Strictly preserve the provided normalization for output.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality_control\\\": {\\n        \\\"procedures\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high mitochondrial fraction\\\",\\n          \\\"Remove doublets if scores available\\\",\\n          \\\"Filter perturbations with too few cells for stable mean estimation (but keep for training if possible)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"n_genes > 200 (adjust based on distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"pct_mito < 20% (adjust based on distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet score threshold\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Compute QC metrics from raw counts if available; otherwise from normalized X (less ideal). Use scanpy.pp.calculate_qc_metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that QC does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure remaining cells retain expected perturbation effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_qc\\\": \\\"No decision support. QC thresholds should be data-driven (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD).\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feature_selection\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output\\\",\\n          \\\"For modeling, consider highly variable genes (HVGs) or genes with nonzero expression in controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"HVG top 2000-5000 for model input\\\",\\n          \\\"Exclude genes with zero variance in controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes on control cells or all cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that HVGs include known perturbation-responsive genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in feature space (they must be, since output requires all genes).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"No decision support. Feature selection is optional for model efficiency; output must cover all genes.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"batch_correction\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Include batch as covariate in model\\\",\\n          \\\"ComBat or Harmony if batch labels available and effects strong\\\",\\n          \\\"Regress out technical covariates (e.g., cell cycle) if appropriate\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Significant association between batch and perturbation assignment\\\",\\n          \\\"Visual inspection of batch effects (e.g., PCA)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Check obs columns for batch/replicate. Use scanpy or scvi-tools for integration if needed.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after correction; ensure biological effects preserved.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Known perturbation effects should remain consistent across batches.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_batch\\\": \\\"No decision support. Batch correction is risky if batch is confounded with perturbation; verify design first.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Leave-perturbation-out cross-validation\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate pearson_delta on held-out training perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Check top-20 DE gene recovery\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta > 0.3 (expected for good models)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC > 0.5\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Split training perturbations into folds; train on all but one fold; evaluate on held-out fold using same metrics as evaluator.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Ensure cross-validation split mimics unseen-perturbation setting (no cell leakage).\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted effects for known regulators are biologically plausible.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support. Cross-validation is essential given no direct validation set.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Check prediction shape and order\\\",\\n          \\\"Verify no NaN/Inf in predictions\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare prediction distribution to training mean distribution\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"means shape == (n_perts, n_genes)\\\",\\n          \\\"perts order matches heldout_perturbations.json\\\",\\n          \\\"Predictions within reasonable range of training expression\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load predictions.npz and assert shapes and order.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run a dummy submission with control mean to ensure evaluator accepts format.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure float dtype and no compression issues.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support. Format compliance is critical.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"quality_assessment\\\": {\\n    \\\"completeness\\\": {\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"Training file contains many perturbations but held-out perturbations have zero cells. Coverage of gene space is complete (all genes required in output).\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_data\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbations entirely missing from training (by design). Some genes may be zero-inflated or undetected in some perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Completeness is adequate for training but generalization to unseen perturbations is the key challenge.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use gene-level models that generalize across perturbations (e.g., linear models on gene features, baseline control mean plus predicted delta)\\\",\\n        \\\"Leverage similarity between perturbations (e.g., pathway/network information) if available\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_completeness\\\": \\\"No decision support. The unseen-perturbation setting requires models that do not rely on perturbation identity.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": \\\"K562 CRISPRi screen is a standard perturbation dataset; gene expression readout is biologically relevant.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical\\\": \\\"Normalization and gene space are relevant to the evaluator's metrics.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Data is relevant for the task; main risk is distribution shift to unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that training perturbations cover diverse gene functions.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Known biology of K562 (erythroleukemia) should inform expectations.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_relevance\\\": \\\"No decision support. Relevance is high given matched cell line and perturbation type.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n      \\\"technical\\\": \\\"Normalization is fixed; reproducibility depends on model training and random seeds.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological\\\": \\\"Biological reproducibility across cells within a perturbation can be estimated from training data.\\\",\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Technical reproducibility should be high if pipeline is deterministic; biological reproducibility varies.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run model with multiple seeds and\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset is highly relevant: K562 CRISPRi Perturb-seq with scRNA-seq readout directly matches the prediction task of unseen-perturbation mean expression. The biological context (chronic myeloid leukemia cell line, CRISPRi knockdown) is well-defined and appropriate for testing generalization via gene function and regulatory relationships.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The dataset analysis assumes K562 CRISPRi but does not verify cell line, perturbation type, or control labels from the actual h5ad metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological relevance is asserted without confirming that training perturbations cover diverse pathways and essential genes, which is critical for transfer to held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit check of on-target knockdown efficiency or off-target effects in training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential confounding of perturbation effects with cell cycle, culture state, or copy-number alterations in K562 is not assessed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Verify experimental design directly from h5ad obs columns: perturbation labels, control labels, batch/replicate, cell cycle scores, and QC metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway and functional diversity of training perturbations (e.g., GO enrichment, essential gene overlap) to estimate coverage for held-out targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute target gene knockdown efficiency per training perturbation and flag perturbations with weak or absent on-target effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Assess association between perturbation labels and technical covariates (batch, library size, cell cycle) to identify confounding.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that control cells are correctly labeled and representative; check that training perturbations include a range of essential and non-essential genes; verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use known K562 biology (e.g., erythroleukemia markers, essential gene phenotypes) to sanity-check control baseline and perturbation effects; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty, so all assumptions must be explicitly verified against the h5ad metadata. Align dataset relevance assessment with the actual data by documenting metadata checks and any deviations from the assumed design.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes that mean expression is the prediction target and that perturbation effects should be sparse and pathway-mediated. However, interpretability is discussed mainly at the model level rather than dataset level; the dataset itself supports interpretability through gene identities and potential pathway annotations, but these are not yet leveraged.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No concrete plan to map gene identifiers to functional annotations (pathways, networks) for interpretability and transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to handle target genes of held-out perturbations in the output gene space, which is essential for biological interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential batch or cell-state effects could obscure interpretable perturbation signatures if not addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) to support feature construction and post-hoc interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space, even if not expressed in training, to allow on-target effect interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Plan to report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with pathway enrichment to facilitate biological interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that gene identifiers are consistent and that pathway annotations cover a substantial fraction of genes; verify that predicted top genes for validation perturbations are enriched for relevant pathways.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For selected validation perturbations, compare predicted signatures to known CRISPRi phenotypes (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown should downregulate translation machinery).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; interpretability efforts should be documented as assumptions and validated against known biology where possible.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly identifies standard scRNA-seq QC metrics and artifacts (doublets, ambient RNA, batch effects). However, it does not confirm which QC metrics are available in the h5ad and whether raw counts are accessible for proper QC.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"QC thresholds are suggested but not derived from the actual data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"It is unclear whether raw counts are available; if only normalized X is present, mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit plan to check for batch confounding with perturbation labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell numbers per perturbation are not yet quantified, which affects mean stability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect h5ad layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use data-driven QC thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD) and verify that filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify cells per perturbation and flag low-cell perturbations for shrinkage or exclusion from training.\\\",\\n          \\\"Test for association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, avoid aggressive batch correction and instead include batch as covariate where possible.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable; check that no perturbation is entirely lost.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Verify that normalization (CP10K + log1p) is already applied and that QC does not alter the output space; confirm gene order and identifiers match evaluator expectations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; quality assessment should be data-driven and documented, with explicit checks of metadata availability.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis emphasizes deterministic pipelines and fixed normalization, which is appropriate. However, reproducibility is not fully specified: random seeds, software versions, and data splits are not detailed.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit seed management or version pinning for anndata, scanpy, scikit-learn, etc.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cross-validation splits are not defined in a reproducible manner.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks) is not addressed.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to archive intermediate outputs (control mean, perturbation means) for audit.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps and record software versions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define and save cross-validation folds (e.g., by perturbation ID) to ensure reproducibility.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with fixed solver) and document any non-deterministic components.\\\",\\n          \\\"Save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients) for reproducibility.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Check that output format (npz keys, shapes, gene order) is exactly as required and reproducible across runs.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; reproducibility requirements should be explicitly stated and validated as part of the pipeline.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Preprocessing recommendations are generally sound: preserve provided normalization, perform QC, consider HVGs for modeling, and be cautious with batch correction. However, recommendations are not yet grounded in the actual data properties.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"QC thresholds and HVG selection are suggested without inspecting the data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction is recommended conditionally but the risk of confounding with perturbation is not fully resolved.\\\",\\n          \\\"Feature selection for modeling is optional but no guidance on how to handle genes with zero expression in controls.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit plan for handling target genes of held-out perturbations during feature selection.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect distributions of n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito (if available) and set thresholds accordingly; document rationale.\\\",\\n          \\\"Decide on batch correction only after checking confounding; prefer including batch as covariate in models if confounded.\\\",\\n          \\\"For modeling, use HVGs or all genes but ensure output includes all genes; handle zero-variance genes by excluding from model input but retaining in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in any feature selection used for model input, or use a model that can predict them from gene features.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean and perturbation means before and after preprocessing steps; check that known housekeeping genes remain stable.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Use scanpy for QC and HVG selection; store preprocessing parameters and filtered cell/gene lists for reproducibility.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; preprocessing choices should be justified by data inspection and documented as assumptions.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The validation plan for preprocessing is reasonable (leave-perturbation-out CV, format checks) but lacks specific implementation details and baselines for comparison.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation strategy is mentioned but not fully specified (e.g., how many folds, stratification).\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit comparison of preprocessing variants (e.g., with/without QC, with/without batch covariate).\\\",\\n          \\\"Format validation is mentioned but not detailed (e.g., exact keys, shapes, dtype).\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to validate that preprocessing does not introduce leakage (e.g., using held-out perturbation information).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare model performance with and without QC and batch covariates to justify preprocessing choices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps are fit on training data only (e.g., HVG selection, scaling) to avoid leakage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run a dummy submission with control mean to confirm evaluator accepts format; compute metrics on internal CV and compare to baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Write modular validation scripts that can be run independently; log all metrics and preprocessing parameters.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; validation should be designed to mimic the evaluator exactly and to detect preprocessing-induced artifacts.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality assessment is comprehensive in listing potential issues (low cell numbers, variable knockdown, batch effects) but does not quantify them from the data.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No actual quantification of cells per perturbation, knockdown efficiency, or batch confounding.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quality issues are listed generically without prioritization based on data.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to handle perturbations with very few cells in training.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cell quality relative to perturbed cells is not assessed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Compute and report per-perturbation cell counts, target gene expression reduction, and within-perturbation variance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize quality issues based on their expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a minimum cell threshold for including perturbations in training; consider shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells to detect quality imbalances.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling; compare control mean across batches.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Use scanpy and custom scripts to compute quality metrics; store results in a table for review.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_assessment\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; quality assessment should be data-driven and documented with quantitative summaries.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"Quality validation is mentioned (compare known essential gene knockdowns, check QC filtering) but lacks concrete metrics and thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific known essential genes or pathways identified for validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"No quantitative criteria for acceptable knockdown efficiency or mean stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to validate that QC filtering preserves biological signal.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Select a set of known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define quantitative thresholds for mean stability (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9).\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after QC; compute correlation and effect size changes.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement subsampling-based stability analysis and known-gene checks in the pipeline.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; validation should be tied to biological expectations and data-driven stability metrics.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"problem\\\": {\\n    \\\"definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem scope is well-defined: predict mean expression for unseen CRISPRi perturbations in K562 using only provided training data. Biological and technical scopes are appropriately bounded, and limitations are acknowledged.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Scope assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations, which may not hold if batches differ.\\\",\\n          \\\"Scope does not explicitly address how to handle perturbations targeting genes with no expression in K562.\\\",\\n          \\\"The assumption that mean expression is a meaningful summary may fail for bimodal responses.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add explicit checks for control representativeness across batches and consider batch-matched controls if available.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a strategy for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes (e.g., predict near-control mean with shrinkage).\\\",\\n          \\\"Acknowledge bimodal responses and consider evaluating distributional metrics if possible, though output is mean.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify control representativeness by comparing control means across batches; check target gene expression for held-out perturbations if metadata allows.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure scope aligns with known K562 biology (e.g., copy-number effects) and that predictions are biologically plausible.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_scope\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; scope assumptions should be explicitly stated and tested where possible.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clarity\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition is clear and formal, with well-specified input/output mapping and evaluation criteria. However, some assumptions (e.g., perturbation effects mediated by target gene function) are stated without justification.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumption that perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function may not hold for all genes (e.g., off-target effects).\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit definition of how to compute delta from control (e