itive shifts in expression space.\"\n        ],\n        \"limitations\": [\n          \"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects; they may underfit complex regulatory responses.\",\n          \"Control-mean baseline ignores perturbation-specific effects entirely and is only useful as a sanity check.\",\n          \"Autoencoder-based methods often require large training sets of perturbations and may overfit to seen perturbations; generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging.\",\n          \"Graph neural networks depend heavily on the quality and completeness of prior networks; K562-specific regulatory networks may be incomplete.\",\n          \"Foundation models are computationally expensive, may have data leakage concerns, and often require fine-tuning to achieve competitive performance on specific screens.\",\n          \"Most methods predict single-cell distributions or pseudobulk, but the task requires mean expression; model outputs may need careful aggregation.\",\n          \"Evaluation metrics (e.g., Pearson delta) can be dominated by highly expressed genes, and top-20 DE gene metrics are sensitive to noise.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"Perturbation prediction models should capture biologically meaningful gene programs (e.g., CRISPRi knockdown effects on essential genes, pathway responses). Simple linear/additive models reflect the assumption that perturbation effects are approximately additive in log-normalized space, which is a reasonable first-order approximation. Graph-based and foundation models aim to encode regulatory relationships, but their biological interpretability is often limited. For K562 CRISPRi, the most relevant signals are likely direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses; models that explicitly model gene-gene interactions or use pathway priors may be more biologically grounded.\"\n      },\n      \"model_comparison\": {\n        \"comparison_criteria\": [\n          \"Predictive accuracy on unseen perturbations (Pearson delta, MSE, top-20 DE metrics)\",\n          \"Generalization ability to held-out perturbations and cell types\",\n          \"Biological interpretability of learned representations and predictions\",\n          \"Computational efficiency (training time, inference time, memory)\",\n          \"Data efficiency (performance as a function of training set size)\",\n          \"Robustness to biological and technical noise (batch effects, stochasticity)\",\n          \"Ease of implementation and reproducibility\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta (change from control) per perturbation\",\n          \"Mean squared error (MSE) on mean expression\",\n          \"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 on top-20 differentially expressed genes\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene rankings\",\n          \"Area under the precision-recall curve for identifying differentially expressed genes\",\n          \"Cosine similarity between predicted and true perturbation effect vectors\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": \"Linear models offer direct interpretability via coefficients that can be mapped to gene effects. Graph-based models can highlight pathways and gene modules. Foundation models are generally black-box, though attention weights may provide some insight. For biological validation, predicted top DE genes should be compared to known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes leads to growth arrest signatures).\",\n        \"computational_requirements\": \"Linear baselines require minimal compute (seconds to minutes on CPU). Autoencoder and graph models require GPUs and hours of training. Foundation models require significant GPU memory and may need fine-tuning, which can take days. For this task, given the dataset size and evaluation constraints, simple baselines are likely sufficient and more practical.\"\n      },\n      \"recommendations\": {\n        \"model_selection\": [\n          \"Start with strong simple baselines: control-mean, linear regression (Ridge) from perturbation embeddings (e.g., one-hot or learned embeddings) to mean expression, and effect-size transfer.\",\n          \"Implement a latent linear model (PCA on control cells + regression) to capture major expression axes.\",\n          \"If resources permit, evaluate a graph-based model (GEARS) or a lightweight autoencoder (scGen) for comparison.\",\n          \"Avoid heavy foundation models unless zero-shot performance is competitive and computational budget allows.\"\n        ],\n        \"implementation_priority\": [\n          \"First, establish control-mean and linear regression baselines to set a performance floor.\",\n          \"Second, implement effect-size transfer and PCA-based regression.\",\n          \"Third, if time permits, implement a simple neural network (MLP) on perturbation embeddings.\",\n          \"Focus on robust cross-validation and evaluation using the specified metrics.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Validate predictions by checking whether known K562 CRISPRi effects are recovered (e.g., knockdown of ribosomal genes leads to downregulation of translation machinery). Compare predicted top DE genes with known pathways. Use gene set enrichment analysis on predicted effect vectors to assess biological plausibility.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"evaluation_framework\": {\n      \"metrics\": {\n        \"primary_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control\",\n          \"MSE on mean expression across all genes\",\n          \"MSE on top-20 DE genes (per perturbation, averaged)\"\n        ],\n        \"secondary_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson correlation on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"R2 on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\",\n          \"Cosine similarity between predicted and true delta vectors\",\n          \"Mean absolute error (MAE)\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_metrics\": [\n          \"Recovery of known pathway signatures (e.g., GSEA on predicted deltas)\",\n          \"Precision-recall for identifying true DE genes\",\n          \"Correlation with known essential gene knockdown signatures\",\n          \"Conservation of gene-gene co-expression patterns in predicted profiles\"\n        ],\n        \"validation_metrics\": [\n          \"Cross-validation performance across held-out perturbations\",\n          \"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits\",\n          \"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\",\n          \"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"validation_strategy\": {\n        \"cross_validation\": \"Use leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation on the training set to estimate generalization to unseen perturbations. Given the held-out set is fixed, we can simulate the unseen-perturbation setting by holding out perturbations from training. Stratify by perturbation strength or pathway to ensure representative splits.\",\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Assess biological plausibility of predictions via pathway enrichment and comparison to known K562 CRISPRi phenotypes. Use independent data (if available) or literature-based signatures to validate top predicted effects.\",\n        \"independent_testing\": \"The provided held-out perturbations serve as an independent test set. Ensure no data leakage: models must not see any cells from held-out perturbations during training. Use only the provided input file for training.\",\n        \"robustness_testing\": \"Evaluate model performance under different normalization schemes, random gene subsampling, and noise addition. Test sensitivity to hyperparameters and random initialization.\"\n      },\n      \"benchmarks\": {\n        \"baseline_models\": [\n          \"Control-mean predictor (predicts control mean for all perturbations)\",\n          \"Linear regression (Ridge) with one-hot perturbation encoding\",\n          \"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen)\",\n          \"PCA-based linear model (PCA on control, regression on perturbation)\"\n        ],\n        \"state_of_the_art\": [\n          \"GEARS (graph-enhanced perturbation prediction)\",\n          \"scGen (autoencoder-based)\",\n          \"CPA (compositional perturbation autoencoder)\",\n          \"Foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) if applicable\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_benchmarks\": [\n          \"Known K562 CRISPRi effects from literature (e.g., knockdown of essential genes)\",\n          \"Pathway-level concordance with known perturbation signatures\",\n          \"Gene-gene correlation structure preservation\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_targets\": [\n          \"Pearson delta > 0.3 (indicative of meaningful signal)\",\n          \"Top-20 DE MSE lower than control-mean baseline\",\n          \"R2 on top-20 DE genes > 0.1\",\n          \"Performance significantly better than control-mean baseline (p < 0.05 via permutation test)\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"performance_analysis\": {\n      \"model_performance\": {\n        \"accuracy_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson delta per perturbation (mean and distribution)\",\n          \"MSE across all genes\",\n          \"Top-20 DE MSE, Pearson, R2\",\n          \"Spearman correlation of gene effects\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"Models that capture direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses will show higher Pearson delta. Simple linear models may perform well if perturbation effects are additive in log space. Biological relevance can be assessed by enrichment of predicted top genes in known K562 pathways.\",\n        \"generalization\": \"Generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging. Linear models may generalize if perturbations share additive effects. Graph-based models may generalize better by leveraging gene network similarity. Performance should be evaluated via leave-one-perturbation-out CV.\",\n        \"robustness\": \"Models should be robust to technical noise (dropout, batch effects) and biological variability. Regularized linear models are typically robust. Neural networks may overfit and require careful regularization.\"\n      },\n      \"comparative_analysis\": {\n        \"baseline_comparison\": \"Control-mean baseline will have near-zero Pearson delta and high MSE. Linear regression and effect-size transfer are expected to outperform it. The gap between linear and more complex models indicates the extent of nonlinearity in perturbation effects.\",\n        \"state_of_the_art_comparison\": \"State-of-the-art models like GEARS may outperform linear baselines on unseen perturbations if gene network priors are informative. However, given the small size of many perturbation screens, simple models often remain competitive. Foundation models may be strong zero-shot but require significant resources.\",\n        \"biological_comparison\": \"Predictions should be compared at the pathway level: models that recover known K562 CRISPRi signatures (e.g., ribosome biogenesis downregulation upon knockdown of ribosomal genes) are more biologically valid.\",\n        \"computational_comparison\": \"Linear models are orders of magnitude faster and require less memory. The performance gain of complex models must justify the computational cost.\"\n      },\n      \"strengths_weaknesses\": {\n        \"strengths\": [\n          \"Simple linear models are interpretable, fast, and robust.\",\n          \"Effect-size transfer leverages seen perturbations and can generalize to similar perturbations.\",\n          \"Graph-based models incorporate biological priors and may capture pathway-level effects.\",\n          \"Ensemble approaches can combine strengths of multiple models.\"\n        ],\n        \"weaknesses\": [\n          \"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial effects.\",\n          \"Complex models may overfit to seen perturbations and fail on unseen ones.\",\n          \"Evaluation metrics may be dominated by highly expressed genes; top-20 DE metrics are noisy.\",\n          \"Limited training data for unseen perturbations makes generalization difficult.\",\n          \"Biological interpretability of deep models is limited.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_insights\": [\n          \"CRISPRi perturbations often produce subtle expression changes; effect sizes may be small.\",\n          \"Essential gene knockdowns may induce stress responses that dominate expression changes.\",\n          \"Gene-gene correlations in control cells provide a baseline for expected co-expression.\",\n          \"Pathway-level analysis can reveal whether models capture coordinated responses.\"\n        ],\n        \"technical_limitations\": [\n          \"Single-cell data sparsity and dropout can obscure true mean expression.\",\n          \"Normalization and log transformation may distort additive effects.\",\n          \"Batch effects and technical variability can confound predictions.\",\n          \"Limited number of perturbations in training set restricts model complexity.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"improvement_suggestions\": {\n      \"model_enhancements\": {\n        \"architectural_improvements\": [\n          \"Incorporate gene-gene interaction networks (e.g., from STRING or K562-specific networks) into models.\",\n          \"Use attention mechanisms to weight genes by relevance to perturbation.\",\n          \"Develop hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections.\",\n          \"Explore multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength.\"\n        ],\n        \"training_optimizations\": [\n          \"Use regularization (L2, dropout) to prevent overfitting on small perturbation sets.\",\n          \"Employ data augmentation (e.g., noise injection, gene dropout) to improve robustness.\",\n          \"Use cross-validation for hyperparameter tuning.\",\n          \"Consider transfer learning from larger perturbation screens (if allowed, but task restricts to provided data).\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": [\n          \"Incorporate pathway annotations as features or constraints.\",\n          \"Use sparse models to identify key genes driving predictions.\",\n          \"Post-hoc interpretability via gene set enrichment on model weights or predictions.\",\n          \"Validate predictions against known CRISPRi phenotypes.\"\n        ],\n        \"computational_efficiency\": [\n          \"Use dimensionality reduction (PCA) before regression to reduce parameters.\",\n          \"Implement efficient linear solvers for Ridge regression.\",\n          \"Parallelize cross-validation and hyperparameter search.\",\n          \"Use GPU acceleration only for complex models when necessary.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"implementation_optimizations\": {\n        \"code_optimizations\": [\n          \"Vectorize operations and use efficient data structures (e.g., sparse matrices for one-hot encoding).\",\n          \"Cache intermediate results (e.g., control mean, PCA components).\",\n          \"Use memory-efficient data loading for large h5ad files.\"\n        ],\n        \"pipeline_improvements\": [\n          \"Automate cross-validation and metric computation.\",\n          \"Implement logging and experiment tracking.\",\n          \"Ensure reproducibility with fixed random seeds.\",\n          \"Create modular code for easy swapping of models and metrics.\"\n        ],\n        \"validation_enhancements\": [\n          \"Use nested cross-validation for unbiased performance estimation.\",\n          \"Perform statistical significance testing against baselines.\",\n          \"Assess prediction uncertainty (e.g., via bootstrap).\",\n          \"Validate on multiple data splits to ensure stability.\"\n        ],\n        \"deployment_considerations\": \"For this task, deployment is not required, but the pipeline should be reproducible and efficient. Predictions should be saved in the specified format with correct gene order and normalization. Ensure no data leakage and that only the provided input file is used for training.\"\n      },\n      \"future_work\": {\n        \"research_directions\": [\n          \"Develop models that explicitly model perturbation mechanisms (e.g., CRISPRi knockdown efficiency).\",\n          \"Integrate multi-omics data (e.g., chromatin accessibility) to improve prediction.\",\n          \"Explore causal inference approaches for perturbation effects.\",\n          \"Benchmark across multiple cell lines and perturbation types.\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_validation\": [\n          \"Experimental validation of predicted top gene effects via qPCR or RNA-seq.\",\n          \"Use CRISPRi with multiple guides per gene to assess reproducibility.\",\n          \"Compare predictions to independent perturbation screens (e.g., DepMap).\",\n          \"Perform pathway-level validation using reporter assays.\"\n        ],\n        \"clinical_translation\": [\n          \"Perturbation prediction can inform drug target discovery and mechanism of action.\",\n          \"Models could be adapted to predict drug responses in patient-derived cells.\",\n          \"Need for robust generalization across cell types and genetic backgrounds.\"\n        ],\n        \"technical_advancements\": [\n          \"Leverage single-cell foundation models with improved zero-shot capabilities.\",\n          \"Develop scalable graph neural networks for large gene networks.\",\n          \"Incorporate uncertainty quantification for reliable predictions.\",\n          \"Improve normalization methods for perturbation screens.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset is highly relevant: K562 CRISPRi Perturb-seq with scRNA-seq readout directly matches the prediction task of unseen-perturbation mean expression. The biological context (chronic myeloid leukemia cell line, CRISPRi knockdown) is well-defined and appropriate for testing generalization via gene function and regulatory relationships.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The dataset analysis assumes K562 CRISPRi but does not verify cell line, perturbation type, or control labels from the actual h5ad metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological relevance is asserted without confirming that training perturbations cover diverse pathways and essential genes, which is critical for transfer to held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit check of on-target knockdown efficiency or off-target effects in training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential confounding of perturbation effects with cell cycle, culture state, or copy-number alterations in K562 is not assessed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Verify experimental design directly from h5ad obs columns: perturbation labels, control labels, batch/replicate, cell cycle scores, and QC metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway and functional diversity of training perturbations (e.g., GO enrichment, essential gene overlap) to estimate coverage for held-out targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute target gene knockdown efficiency per training perturbation and flag perturbations with weak or absent on-target effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Assess association between perturbation labels and technical covariates (batch, library size, cell cycle) to identify confounding.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that control cells are correctly labeled and representative; check that training perturbations include a range of essential and non-essential genes; verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use known K562 biology (e.g., erythroleukemia markers, essential gene phenotypes) to sanity-check control baseline and perturbation effects; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty, so all assumptions must be explicitly verified against the h5ad metadata. Align dataset relevance assessment with the actual data by documenting metadata checks and any deviations from the assumed design.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes that mean expression is the prediction target and that perturbation effects should be sparse and pathway-mediated. However, interpretability is discussed mainly at the model level rather than dataset level; the dataset itself supports interpretability through gene identities and potential pathway annotations, but these are not yet leveraged.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No concrete plan to map gene identifiers to functional annotations (pathways, networks) for interpretability and transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to handle target genes of held-out perturbations in the output gene space, which is essential for biological interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential batch or cell-state effects could obscure interpretable perturbation signatures if not addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) to support feature construction and post-hoc interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space, even if not expressed in training, to allow on-target effect interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Plan to report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with pathway enrichment to facilitate biological interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that gene identifiers are consistent and that pathway annotations cover a substantial fraction of genes; verify that predicted top genes for validation perturbations are enriched for relevant pathways.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For selected validation perturbations, compare predicted signatures to known CRISPRi phenotypes (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown should downregulate translation machinery).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; interpretability efforts should be documented as assumptions and validated against known biology where possible.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly identifies standard scRNA-seq QC metrics and artifacts (doublets, ambient RNA, batch effects). However, it does not confirm which QC metrics are available in the h5ad and whether raw counts are accessible for proper QC.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"QC thresholds are suggested but not derived from the actual data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"It is unclear whether raw counts are available; if only normalized X is present, mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit plan to check for batch confounding with perturbation labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell numbers per perturbation are not yet quantified, which affects mean stability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect h5ad layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use data-driven QC thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD) and verify that filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify cells per perturbation and flag low-cell perturbations for shrinkage or exclusion from training.\\\",\\n          \\\"Test for association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, avoid aggressive batch correction and instead include batch as covariate where possible.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable; check that no perturbation is entirely lost.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Verify that normalization (CP10K + log1p) is already applied and that QC does not alter the output space; confirm gene order and identifiers match evaluator expectations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; quality assessment should be data-driven and documented, with explicit checks of metadata availability.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis emphasizes deterministic pipelines and fixed normalization, which is appropriate. However, reproducibility is not fully specified: random seeds, software versions, and data splits are not detailed.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit seed management or version pinning for anndata, scanpy, scikit-learn, etc.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cross-validation splits are not defined in a reproducible manner.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks) is not addressed.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to archive intermediate outputs (control mean, perturbation means) for audit.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps and record software versions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define and save cross-validation folds (e.g., by perturbation ID) to ensure reproducibility.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with fixed solver) and document any non-deterministic components.\\\",\\n          \\\"Save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients) for reproducibility.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Check that output format (npz keys, shapes, gene order) is exactly as required and reproducible across runs.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; reproducibility requirements should be explicitly stated and validated as part of the pipeline.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Preprocessing recommendations are generally sound: preserve provided normalization, perform QC, consider HVGs for modeling, and be cautious with batch correction. However, recommendations are not yet grounded in the actual data properties.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"QC thresholds and HVG selection are suggested without inspecting the data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction is recommended conditionally but the risk of confounding with perturbation is not fully resolved.\\\",\\n          \\\"Feature selection for modeling is optional but no guidance on how to handle genes with zero expression in controls.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit plan for handling target genes of held-out perturbations during feature selection.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect distributions of n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito (if available) and set thresholds accordingly; document rationale.\\\",\\n          \\\"Decide on batch correction only after checking confounding; prefer including batch as covariate in models if confounded.\\\",\\n          \\\"For modeling, use HVGs or all genes but ensure output includes all genes; handle zero-variance genes by excluding from model input but retaining in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in any feature selection used for model input, or use a model that can predict them from gene features.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean and perturbation means before and after preprocessing steps; check that known housekeeping genes remain stable.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Use scanpy for QC and HVG selection; store preprocessing parameters and filtered cell/gene lists for reproducibility.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; preprocessing choices should be justified by data inspection and documented as assumptions.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The validation plan for preprocessing is reasonable (leave-perturbation-out CV, format checks) but lacks specific implementation details and baselines for comparison.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation strategy is mentioned but not fully specified (e.g., how many folds, stratification).\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit comparison of preprocessing variants (e.g., with/without QC, with/without batch covariate).\\\",\\n          \\\"Format validation is mentioned but not detailed (e.g., exact keys, shapes, dtype).\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to validate that preprocessing does not introduce leakage (e.g., using held-out perturbation information).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare model performance with and without QC and batch covariates to justify preprocessing choices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps are fit on training data only (e.g., HVG selection, scaling) to avoid leakage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run a dummy submission with control mean to confirm evaluator accepts format; compute metrics on internal CV and compare to baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Write modular validation scripts that can be run independently; log all metrics and preprocessing parameters.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; validation should be designed to mimic the evaluator exactly and to detect preprocessing-induced artifacts.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality assessment is comprehensive in listing potential issues (low cell numbers, variable knockdown, batch effects) but does not quantify them from the data.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No actual quantification of cells per perturbation, knockdown efficiency, or batch confounding.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quality issues are listed generically without prioritization based on data.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to handle perturbations with very few cells in training.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cell quality relative to perturbed cells is not assessed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Compute and report per-perturbation cell counts, target gene expression reduction, and within-perturbation variance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize quality issues based on their expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a minimum cell threshold for including perturbations in training; consider shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells to detect quality imbalances.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling; compare control mean across batches.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Use scanpy and custom scripts to compute quality metrics; store results in a table for review.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_assessment\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; quality assessment should be data-driven and documented with quantitative summaries.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"Quality validation is mentioned (compare known essential gene knockdowns, check QC filtering) but lacks concrete metrics and thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific known essential genes or pathways identified for validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"No quantitative criteria for acceptable knockdown efficiency or mean stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to validate that QC filtering preserves biological signal.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Select a set of known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define quantitative thresholds for mean stability (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9).\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after QC; compute correlation and effect size changes.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement subsampling-based stability analysis and known-gene checks in the pipeline.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; validation should be tied to biological expectations and data-driven stability metrics.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"problem\\\": {\\n    \\\"definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem scope is well-defined: predict mean expression for unseen CRISPRi perturbations in K562 using only provided training data. Biological and technical scopes are appropriately bounded, and limitations are acknowledged.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Scope assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations, which may not hold if batches differ.\\\",\\n          \\\"Scope does not explicitly address how to handle perturbations targeting genes with no expression in K562.\\\",\\n          \\\"The assumption that mean expression is a meaningful summary may fail for bimodal responses.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add explicit checks for control representativeness across batches and consider batch-matched controls if available.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a strategy for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes (e.g., predict near-control mean with shrinkage).\\\",\\n          \\\"Acknowledge bimodal responses and consider evaluating distributional metrics if possible, though output is mean.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify control representativeness by comparing control means across batches; check target gene expression for held-out perturbations if metadata allows.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure scope aligns with known K562 biology (e.g., copy-number effects) and that predictions are biologically plausible.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_scope\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; scope assumptions should be explicitly stated and tested where possible.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clarity\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition is clear and formal, with well-specified input/output mapping and evaluation criteria. However, some assumptions (e.g., perturbation effects mediated by target gene function) are stated without justification.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumption that perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function may not hold for all genes (e.g., off-target effects).\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit definition of how to compute delta from control (e.g., log-space difference vs. ratio).\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation criteria mention top-20 DE genes but do not specify how DE genes are defined (e.g., from true data).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Clarify that delta is computed in the same normalized log1p space as the output, and specify the formula.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define top-20 DE genes as those with largest absolute delta in true held-out data, as per evaluator.\\\",\\n          \\\"Acknowledge off-target effects as a limitation and consider robustness to them.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Ensure all team members interpret delta and DE genes consistently; document definitions.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that the definition of delta aligns with biological interpretation of knockdown effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_clarity\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; clarity should be ensured through explicit definitions and documentation.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feasibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem is feasible with simple models and the provided data, but generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging. The analysis correctly identifies computational and data limitations.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Feasibility depends on functional diversity of training perturbations, which is not yet quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational resources are assumed but not verified.\\\",\\n          \\\"No fallback plan if simple models fail to beat control baseline.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway coverage