oes not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means for training. Document all QC parameters and filtered cell/gene lists.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"noise\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sources\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cell cycle heterogeneity\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Clonal variation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stochastic gene expression\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Increases variance of perturbation means, especially for low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Estimate by comparing within-perturbation variance to between-perturbation variance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Use robust mean estimation (e.g., trimmed mean)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Model cell-level covariates (cell cycle) if available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_noise\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Biological noise is irreducible but can be averaged out with sufficient cells.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sources\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Library size variation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ambient RNA\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sequencing depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Can bias perturbation means if correlated with perturbation assignment.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Normalization (already applied as counts per 10k + log1p)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch correction or covariate adjustment (prefer covariate if confounding possible)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ambient RNA correction (e.g., SoupX) if raw counts available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare means before and after batch correction; ensure biological effects preserved.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Since normalization is already applied, focus on batch effects and cell quality.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"preprocessing\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"normalization\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"strategies\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"recommendations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Use the provided counts per 10k + log1p normalization as-is for both training and prediction output\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Do not re-normalize if it would break compatibility with the evaluator\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The evaluator expects predictions in the same normalized space as the input; altering normalization would invalidate scoring.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"implementation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Load h5ad, use X as normalized expression. For modeling, optionally standardize features per gene (z-score) but inverse-transform predictions to the original space. All preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline; verify known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Ensure known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_normalization\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Strictly preserve the provided normalization for output.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"quality_control\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"procedures\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high mitochondrial fraction using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Remove doublets if scores available\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"problem_definition\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"formal_definition\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_context\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"We predict the transcriptome-wide mean expression response of K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells to CRISPRi knockdown of individual genes (perturbations) not observed during training. Training data are single-cell RNA-seq profiles from K562 cells under many CRISPRi perturbations and controls. Held-out perturbations target unseen genes; the model must generalize from gene-level features and perturbation structure to unseen knockdowns, producing mean log1p CP10K expression for all genes in the same order and normalization as the input. All assumptions about cell line, perturbation type, and control labels must be verified directly from h5ad metadata before modeling.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"input_output_mapping\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Input: training scRNA-seq matrix X (cells x genes) with perturbation labels and control cells; held-out perturbation list P_held (n_perts); gene-level metadata (gene identifiers, optionally embeddings/annotations derivable from provided data). Output: means matrix M (n_perts x n_genes) where M[i,j] is the predicted mean log1p CP10K expression of gene j in K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbation P_held[i]. Evaluation compares M to held-out true perturbation means using pearson_delta (Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control per perturbation, computed in log1p CP10K space as predicted_mean - control_mean), overall MSE, and top-20 DE gene MSE/PCC/R2. Top-20 DE genes are defined as those with largest absolute true delta from control in held-out data, as per the evaluator.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions must be non-negative after inverse log1p transform and consistent with CP10K normalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predicted means should reflect biologically plausible expression ranges for K562 genes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects should be sparse and concentrated on biologically related genes/pathways.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control baseline must be estimated from training control cells, not assumed zero; verify control labels and representativeness across batches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are gene-specific and often weak, requiring shrinkage toward control; shrinkage parameter should be tuned by cross-validation over perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no direct training examples; model must rely on gene-gene relationships and functional similarity derivable from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No external data from the same screen may be used; only cellforge_input.h5ad. External pathway annotations may be used only if explicitly permitted; otherwise derive gene features from expression data alone.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562 should be predicted near control mean with strong shrinkage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations and must be treated as a limitation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"evaluation_criteria\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control per perturbation (pearson_delta), where delta = mean_pert - mean_control in log1p CP10K space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and true means across all genes and perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 restricted to the top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation, defined from true held-out data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological plausibility: predicted effects should align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi knockdown direction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Reproducibility: predictions must be generated from a deterministic or seeded pipeline using only provided data, with fixed seeds, pinned software versions, saved cross-validation folds, and archived intermediate artifacts.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"success_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"pearson_delta above a baseline of zero-effect (control-only) prediction and above simple linear baselines.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"MSE lower than control-only baseline and lower than naive gene-mean baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC and R2 significantly above zero-effect baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Consistent improvement across perturbations rather than driven by a few outliers.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological validation: predicted top genes for known pathways show expected knockdown signatures where prior knowledge exists (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown downregulates translation machinery).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"key_variables\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation target gene identity and its functional role in K562.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Baseline expression of target gene and pathway members; flag targets with zero/near-zero baseline expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships and co-expression modules derived from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown efficiency and off-target effects (quantify for training perturbations; treat as unobserved for held-out).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cell cycle, stress, and culture-state variation in K562 (assess via QC metrics and cell cycle scores if available).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Copy-number alterations and karyotype of K562 affecting expression (acknowledge as confounder; assess via expression patterns if possible).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Single-cell library size and dropout.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch effects across perturbation plates or runs; test association with perturbation labels before correction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Guide RNA representation and multiplicity of infection.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Normalization (CP10K, log1p) and gene filtering; verify normalization is already applied in h5ad.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbation list and gene ordering; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in output and feature space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Train/test split by perturbation (unseen-perturbation setting).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"relationships\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation target gene expression is reduced in its own knockdown cells (on-target effect); quantify for training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Knockdown of a regulator propagates to downstream targets and pathways.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Genes with similar functions or co-expression tend to have correlated perturbation responses.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean expression is the baseline from which deltas are computed.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may share pathway membership with training perturbations, enabling transfer; quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Only cellforge_input.h5ad may be used for training.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No held-out perturbation cells are available for training or fine-tuning.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Output must match gene order and normalization exactly; implement automated format checks (keys 'perts' and 'means', shapes, dtype).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions are means, not single-cell distributions; bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Computational resources should be reasonable for a single-machine pipeline (at least 32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hold out a subset of training perturbations to simulate unseen-perturbation prediction; define folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Compare against control-only and gene-mean baselines.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Report pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics on internal validation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Check biological coherence of predicted top genes for validation perturbations; compare to known essential gene knockdowns (e.g., ribosomal genes).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Ensure deterministic seeds and documented preprocessing; re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) are fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Validate that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"scope\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbations; prediction of mean transcriptional response to unseen gene knockdowns; focus on gene-level mean expression and delta from control. Scope assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations; this must be checked across batches. Perturbations targeting unexpressed genes should be predicted near control mean with strong shrinkage. Bimodal responses are acknowledged as a limitation of mean-based evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Single-cell RNA-seq matrix processing, control baseline estimation, feature construction from gene identity and expression, regression/transfer learning from training perturbations to held-out perturbations, and evaluation with Gene-Mila metrics. Feature representation questions focus on features derivable from provided data (co-expression, target expression) unless external annotations are permitted.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot model single-cell heterogeneity or cell-state-specific responses.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Limited ability to predict strong effects for perturbations with no pathway overlap with training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi efficiency and off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562-specific copy-number and culture effects may confound generalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Evaluation is on means, so sparse or bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Assumption that perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function may not hold for all genes (e.g., off-target effects).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells in training are representative of the control baseline for held-out perturbations; verify by comparing control means across batches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene identifiers are consistent and sufficient to derive functional features; verify coverage of pathway annotations if used.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function and pathway membership.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean expression is a meaningful summary of perturbation response.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Training perturbations cover enough functional diversity to allow transfer to held-out perturbations; quantify pathway and essential gene coverage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"If external pathway annotations are used, they are permitted by task rules; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use internal held-out perturbations to verify that predicted deltas recover known pathway signatures, that on-target knockdown is reflected for target genes when possible, and that top predicted genes are enriched for relevant biological processes. Select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes. Confirm predicted effects for selected perturbations with independent qPCR or targeted RNA-seq where feasible.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"key_challenges\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no close functional analog in training.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are often weak and variable, making deltas hard to predict.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562 has complex copy-number alterations that distort expression and perturbation response.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pathway redundancy and feedback can mask or reverse expected effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects may depend on cell state not captured in mean expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Poor generalization to functionally isolated perturbations and underestimation of true effects, reducing pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics. The most critical challenges are distribution shift to unseen perturbations, weak effects, and noisy means.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use gene functional embeddings and pathway annotations to relate held-out targets to training perturbations only if permitted; otherwise derive co-expression modules from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Shrink predictions toward control to avoid spurious large effects; tune shrinkage parameter by cross-validation over perturbations (e.g., ridge regularization or explicit blending with control mean).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model perturbation effects as low-rank or pathway-level modules rather than independent gene effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Incorporate target gene baseline expression and network centrality as features; predict near control mean with strong shrinkage for targets with zero baseline expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use robust control baseline estimation across batches; prefer including batch as covariate over batch correction when confounding is possible.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Internal leave-perturbation-out validation stratified by pathway and target expression to assess generalization across functional categories. Use pilot experiments to confirm which challenges actually degrade performance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Predictions should respect directionality of known regulatory relationships and avoid biologically implausible large effects for genes not connected to the target. Check that mitigations preserve known biological effects.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"High-dimensional output (thousands of genes) with limited number of perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Sparse single-cell data and dropout affecting mean estimates.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch effects between perturbation groups and controls; test association with perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Need to match exact gene order and normalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Overfitting to training perturbations, noisy control baseline, and poor calibration of predicted means.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Aggregate to pseudo-bulk means per perturbation to reduce noise.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use low-rank regression, factor models, or kernel methods on gene features.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Apply strong regularization and cross-validation over perturbations; define and save folds by perturbation ID.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Estimate control mean robustly using all control cells and batch covariates; compare control mean estimates across batches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Validate output shape, gene order, and normalization against input; implement automated format checks.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Specify fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps, record software versions, use deterministic solvers where possible, and save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cross-validation by perturbation with held-out metric computation and sanity checks on output dimensions and value ranges. Re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"implementation_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and optionally torch; deterministic seeds; pinned software versions; memory sufficient for the full matrix and model (at least 32 GB RAM).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"data_quality\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Variable cell numbers per perturbation; quantify and flag low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-expression genes with high dropout.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential guide-level or batch confounding; test association between batch and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells may differ in quality from perturbed cells; compare QC metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Possible doublets or low-quality cells.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unclear whether raw counts are available; if only normalized X is present, mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Noisy perturbation means and biased control baseline, leading to inaccurate delta predictions. Quality issues should be prioritized by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Filter low-quality cells and genes with very low detection using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD); verify that filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use pseudo-bulk averaging and variance stabilization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Include batch/plate covariates if available and confounding is present.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Robustly estimate control mean with trimming or median-based approaches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Downsample or weight perturbations to avoid domination by large groups.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Define a minimum cell threshold for including perturbations in training; consider shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9). Validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Ensure filtered genes retain key K562 markers and that control baseline matches known expression profiles. Select known essential genes in K562 and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"computational\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Large gene dimension makes full covariance modeling expensive.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Need efficient cross-validation over many perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Balancing model complexity with limited perturbation samples.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Reproducible pipeline for held-out prediction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Long runtimes and risk of overfitting or unstable predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use low-rank or factorized models (e.g., PCA on gene space, reduced-rank regression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Precompute gene features and control mean once.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use efficient linear algebra and sparse matrices where possible.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Limit hyperparameter search with nested cross-validation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Archive intermediate outputs for audit.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Measure runtime and memory, and verify results are stable across seeds.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"resource_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Single workstation with at least 32 GB RAM and multi-core CPU; optional GPU for deep models but not required. Verify available compute and memory before modeling.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"interpretability\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions should be attributable to target gene function and pathway membership.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top predicted genes should be biologically coherent.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model should allow inspection of learned gene-gene relationships.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in output and model feature space to allow on-target effect interpretation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"High-dimensional regression coefficients are hard to interpret.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Latent factor models may obscure biological meaning.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"External pathway annotations may not be permitted; interpretability must rely on provided data where necessary.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"solutions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use pathway-level or module-level parameters that map to biological processes; derive modules from provided expression data if external annotations are not permitted.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Report top predicted genes with pathway enrichment where annotations are available.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Compare predicted signatures to known perturbation signatures where available.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Provide feature importance at gene and pathway level.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check enrichment of predicted top genes for pathways related to the target gene and consistency with known K562 biology. For selected validation perturbations, compare predicted signatures to known CRISPRi phenotypes (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown should downreg\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"baseline_models\\\": {\\n    \\\"literature_review\\\": {\\n      \\\"existing_methods\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-mean baseline (predict control mean for all perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear baseline (Ridge regression from perturbation one-hot or learned embeddings to mean expression)\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen perturbations, e.g., Sci-Plex-style linear effect transfer)\\\",\\n          \\\"Latent linear models (PCA on control cells + regression, e.g., scGen-style linear approximation)\\\",\\n          \\\"Autoencoder-based perturbation prediction (e.g., scGen, CPA)\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph neural network models leveraging gene regulatory networks (e.g., GEARS)\\\",\\n          \\\"Foundation-model zero-shot baselines (e.g., scGPT, Geneformer fine-tuned or prompted)\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor / similarity-based perturbation transfer (e.g., using perturbation embeddings derived from target gene features)\\\",\\n          \\\"Hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections\\\",\\n          \\\"Multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-mean baseline provides a lower bound and reveals how much signal is above biological/technical noise.\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear baselines are highly interpretable, fast, robust in low-data regimes, and often competitive for mean-expression prediction.\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect-size transfer models are simple, biologically motivated (additive shifts in log-normalized space), and can generalize to similar perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Autoencoder and latent-variable models can capture nonlinear expression manifolds and disentangle perturbation effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph-based models (GEARS) incorporate biological prior knowledge (gene-gene relationships) and can generalize to unseen perturbations via pathway similarity.\\\",\\n          \\\"Foundation models leverage large-scale pretraining and may capture general gene-regulatory patterns, enabling zero-shot or few-shot prediction.\\\",\\n          \\\"Hybrid and multi-task approaches can combine interpretability of linear models with nonlinear flexibility and share statistical strength across perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-mean baseline ignores perturbation-specific effects entirely and is only useful as a sanity check.\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects; they may underfit complex regulatory responses.\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect-size transfer assumes additive effects and may fail when perturbations interact or when target genes have context-dependent functions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Autoencoder-based methods often require large training sets of perturbations and may overfit to seen perturbations; generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging.\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph neural networks depend heavily on the quality and completeness of prior networks; K562-specific regulatory networks may be incomplete, and external network data may not be allowed under provided-data-only constraints.\\\",\\n          \\\"Foundation models are computationally expensive, may have data leakage concerns, and often require fine-tuning to achieve competitive performance on specific screens; their zero-shot performance on K562 CRISPRi is uncertain.\\\",\\n          \\\"Most methods predict single-cell distributions or pseudobulk, but the task requires mean expression; model outputs may need careful aggregation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation metrics (e.g., Pearson delta) can be dominated by highly expressed genes, and top-20 DE gene metrics are sensitive to noise.\\\",\\n          \\\"Performance on perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression in K562 is unclear; models may need explicit shrinkage toward control.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"Perturbation prediction models should capture biologically meaningful gene programs (e.g., CRISPRi knockdown effects on essential genes, pathway responses). Simple linear/additive models reflect the assumption that perturbation effects are approximately additive in log-normalized space, a reasonable first-order approximation. Graph-based and foundation models aim to encode regulatory relationships, but their biological interpretability is often limited. For K562 CRISPRi, the most relevant signals are likely direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses; models that explicitly model gene-gene interactions or use pathway priors may be more biologically grounded. However, biological relevance must be verified against actual h5ad metadata: confirm cell line (K562), perturbation type (CRISPRi), control labels, and that training perturbations cover diverse pathways and essential genes. Compute target gene knockdown efficiency per training perturbation and flag weak or absent on-target effects. Assess confounding with cell cycle, culture state, or copy-number alterations. Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) only if external annotations are permitted; otherwise derive gene features from provided expression data (e.g., co-expression, target expression).\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"model_comparison\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Predictive accuracy on unseen perturbations (Pearson delta, MSE, top-20 DE metrics)\\\",\\n          \\\"Generalization ability to held-out perturbations and cell types; quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations to estimate feasibility\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological interpretability of learned representations and predictions; ability to recover known K562 CRISPRi signatures\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational efficiency (training time, inference time, memory); verify available compute and plan efficient implementations\\\",\\n          \\\"Data efficiency (performance as a function of training set size); account for low-cell perturbations via shrinkage or exclusion\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to biological and technical noise (batch effects, stochasticity); test association between batch and perturbation labels\\\",\\n          \\\"Ease of implementation and reproducibility; specify seeds, software versions, and saved CV folds\\\",\\n          \\\"Handling of perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression (e.g., shrinkage toward control)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control (delta computed in log1p space as predicted mean minus control mean)\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean squared error (MSE) on mean expression across all genes\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 on top-20 differentially expressed genes (defined as genes with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\\\",\\n          \\\"Area under the precision-recall curve for identifying differentially expressed genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Cosine similarity between predicted and true perturbation effect vectors\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean absolute error (MAE)\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\",\\n          \\\"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_interpretability\\\": \\\"Linear models offer direct interpretability via coefficients that can be mapped to gene effects. Graph-based models can highlight pathways and gene modules. Foundation models are generally black-box, though attention weights may provide some insight. For biological validation, predicted top DE genes should be compared to known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes leads to growth arrest signatures; ribosomal gene knockdown should downregulate translation machinery). Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space, even if not expressed in training, to allow on-target effect interpretation. Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with pathway enrichment (if external annotations allowed) to facilitate biological interpretation.\\\",\\n        \\\"computational_requirements\\\": \\\"Linear baselines require minimal compute (seconds to minutes on CPU). Autoencoder and graph models require GPUs and hours of training. Foundation models require significant GPU memory and may need fine-tuning, which can take days. For this task, given the dataset size and evaluation constraints, simple baselines are likely sufficient and more practical. Verify available compute and memory; plan for efficient implementations. Use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with fixed solver) and document any non-deterministic components.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Start with strong simple baselines: control-mean, linear regression (Ridge) from perturbation embeddings (e.g., one-hot or learned embeddings) to mean expression, and effect-size transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement a latent linear model (PCA on control cells + regression) to capture major expression axes.\\\",\\n          \\\"If resources permit, evaluate a graph-based model (GEARS) or a lightweight autoencoder (scGen) for comparison; use external networks only if allowed.\\\",\\n          \\\"Avoid heavy foundation models unless zero-shot performance is competitive and computational budget allows.\\\",\\n          \\\"Consider hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections and multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength.\\\",\\n          \\\"Establish a minimum acceptable performance (e.g., pearson_delta > 0) and a fallback to control-mean with shrinkage if simple models fail to beat control baseline.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation_priority\\\": [\\n          \\\"First, establish control-mean and linear regression baselines to set a performance floor; run pilot experiments early to assess feasibility.\\\",\\n          \\\"Second, implement effect-size transfer and PCA-based regression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Third, if time permits, implement a simple neural network (MLP) on perturbation embeddings or a hybrid model.\\\",\\n          \\\"Focus on robust cross-validation and evaluation using the specified metrics; define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression (at least 5 folds if enough perturbations).\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps (e.g., HVG selection, scaling) are fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predictions by checking whether known K562 CRISPRi effects are recovered (e.g., knockdown of ribosomal genes leads to downregulation of translation machinery). Compare predicted top DE genes with known pathways. Use gene set enrichment analysis on predicted effect vectors to assess biological plausibility, if external annotations are allowed; otherwise use co-expression modules derived from control cells. Select a set of known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes. Define quantitative thresholds for mean stability (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9) and validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"evaluation_framework\\\": {\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"primary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control (delta computed in log1p space)\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression across all genes\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on top-20 DE genes (per perturbation, averaged; DE genes defined as those with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"secondary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation on top-20 DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\\\",\\n          \\\"Cosine similarity between predicted and true delta vectors\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean absolute error (MAE)\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Recovery of known pathway signatures (e.g., GSEA on predicted deltas, if external annotations allowed; otherwise co-expression module enrichment)\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision-recall for identifying true DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Correlation with known essential gene knockdown signatures (e.g., ribosomal genes in K562)\\\",\\n          \\\"Conservation of gene-gene co-expression patterns in predicted profiles\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation performance across held-out perturbations (leave-one-perturbation-out; at least 5 folds if enough perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\",\\n          \\\"Statistical significance testing against baselines (e.g., permutation test)\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation_strategy\\\": {\\n        \\\"cross_validation\\\": \\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation on the training set to estimate generalization to unseen perturbations. Define and save CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression to ensure representative splits; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations. Given the held-out set is fixed, simulate the unseen-perturbation setting by holding out perturbations from training. Compare model performance with and without QC and batch covariates to justify preprocessing choices. Use nested cross-validation for unbiased performance estimation.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Assess biological plausibility of predictions via pathway enrichment (if external annotations allowed) and comparison to known K562 CRISPRi phenotypes. Use independent data (if available) or literature-based signatures to validate top predicted effects. Select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check expected expression changes. Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space.\\\",\\n        \\\"independent_testing\\\": \\\"The provided held-out perturbations serve as an independent test set. Ensure no data leakage: models must not see any cells from held-out perturbations during training. Use only the provided input file for training. Verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training. Run a dummy submission with control mean to confirm evaluator accepts format.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness_testing\\\": \\\"Evaluate model performance under different normalization schemes, random gene subsampling, and noise addition. Test sensitivity to hyperparameters and random initialization. Use fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps and record software versions. Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability. Save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients) for reproducibility.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"benchmarks\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_models\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-mean predictor (predicts control mean for all perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear regression (Ridge) with one-hot perturbation encoding\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen)\\\",\\n          \\\"PCA-based linear model (PCA on control, regression on perturbation)\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrinkage estimator blending control mean with effect-size transfer, with cross-validated shrinkage parameter\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art\\\": [\\n          \\\"GEARS (graph-enhanced perturbation prediction; use external networks only if allowed)\\\",\\n          \\\"scGen (autoencoder-based)\\\",\\n          \\\"CPA (compositional perturbation autoencoder)\\\",\\n          \\\"Foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) if applicable and computationally feasible\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_benchmarks\\\": [\\n          \\\"Known K562 CRISPRi effects from literature (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, ribosomal genes)\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level concordance with known perturbation signatures (if external annotations allowed; otherwise co-expression modules)\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-gene correlation structure preservation\\\",\\n          \\\"On-target knockdown efficiency per training perturbation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_targets\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson delta > 0.3 (indicative of meaningful signal)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE MSE lower than control-mean baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes > 0.1\\\",\\n          \\\"Performance significantly better than control-mean baseline (p < 0.05 via permutation test)\\\",\\n          \\\"Minimum acceptable performance: pearson_delta > 0; fallback to control-mean with shrinkage if not met\\\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"performance_analysis\\\": {\\n      \\\"model_performance\\\": {\\n        \\\"accuracy_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson delta per perturbation (mean and distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE across all genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE MSE, Pearson, R2\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman correlation of gene effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\",\\n          \\\"Stability across subsamples (e.g., correlation of means > 0.9)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"Models that capture direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses will show higher Pearson delta. Simple linear models may perform well if perturbation effects are additive in log space. Biological relevance can be assessed by enrichment of predicted top genes in known K562 pathways (if external annotations allowed) or co-expression modules. Compute target gene knockdown efficiency per training perturbation and flag weak or absent on-target effects. Assess confounding with cell cycle, culture state, or copy-number alterations.\\\",\\n        \\\"generalization\\\": \\\"Generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging. Linear models may generalize if perturbations share additive effects. Graph-based models may generalize better by leveraging gene network similarity. Performance should be evaluated via leave-one-perturbation-out CV. Quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations to estimate feasibility for held-out targets. Define a strategy for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes (e.g., predict near-control mean with shrinkage).\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness\\\": \\\"Models should be robust to technical noise (dropout, batch effects) and biological variability. Regularized linear models are typically robust. Neural networks may overfit and require careful regularization. Test association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, avoid aggressive batch correction and include batch as covariate where possible. Evaluate model performance under different normalization schemes and noise injection.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"comparative_analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_comparison\\\": \\\"Control-mean baseline will have near-zero Pearson delta and high MSE. Linear regression and effect-size transfer are expected to outperform it. The gap between linear and more complex models indicates the extent of nonlinearity in perturbation effects. Compare model performance with and without QC and batch covariates to justify preprocessing choices.\\\",\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art_comparison\\\": \\\"State-of-the-art models like GEARS may outperform linear baselines on unseen perturbations if gene network priors are informative and allowed. However, given the small size of many perturbation screens, simple models often remain competitive. Foundation models may be strong zero-shot but require significant resources and their zero-shot performance on K562 CRISPRi is uncertain.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_comparison\\\": \\\"Predictions should be compared at the pathway level: models that recover known K562 CRISPRi signatures (e.g., ribosome biogenesis downregulation upon knockdown of ribosomal genes) are more biologically valid. Use known essential genes and literature-based signatures for validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"computational_comparison\\\": \\\"Linear models are orders of magnitude faster and require less memory. The performance gain of complex models must justify the computational cost. Verify available compute and memory; plan for efficient implementations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"strengths_weaknesses\\\": {\\n        \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n          \\\"Simple linear models are interpretable, fast, and robust.\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect-size transfer leverages seen perturbations and can generalize to similar perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph-based models incorporate biological priors and may capture pathway-level effects (if external networks allowed).\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensemble and hybrid approaches can combine strengths of multiple models.\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrinkage estimators provide a principled fallback for weak or unexpressed-target perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"weaknesses\\\": [\\n          \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Complex models may overfit to seen perturbations and fail on unseen ones.\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation metrics may be dominated by highly expressed genes; top-20 DE metrics are noisy.\\\",\\n          \\\"Limited training data for unseen perturbations makes generalization difficult.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological interpretability of deep models is limited.\\\",\\n          \\\"External pathway or network annotations may not be allowed under provided-data-only constraints.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression are challenging.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_insights\\\": [\\n          \\\"CRISPRi perturbations often produce subtle expression changes; effect sizes may be small.\\\",\\n          \\\"Essential gene knockdowns may induce stress responses that dominate expression changes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-gene correlations in control cells provide a baseline for expected co-expression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level analysis can reveal whether models capture coordinated responses.\\\",\\n          \\\"On-target knockdown efficiency varies across perturbations and should be quantified.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical_limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Single-cell data sparsity and dropout can obscure true mean expression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Normalization and log transformation may distort additive effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects and technical variability can confound predictions; test association between batch and perturbation labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"Limited number of perturbations in training set restricts model complexity.\\\",\\n          \\\"QC thresholds should be data-driven (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON