tial genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check expected expression changes; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"decision_support_alignment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Decision support inputs were empty; analysis is based solely on task description and dataset metadata. All assumptions must be explicitly verified against the actual h5ad metadata (obs columns for perturbation, control, batch, cell cycle, QC metrics). Document any deviations from assumed design.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"perturbation\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"design\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) gene knockdown, pooled format. Each perturbation targets one gene; cells with the same sgRNA are grouped as a perturbation. Held-out perturbations have zero cells in the training file (unseen-perturbation setting). Assumption: perturbation labels in h5ad correspond to target genes; verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"types\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Non-targeting control\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"doses\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Single constitutive knockdown (no dose variation expected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"timepoints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Likely a single endpoint (steady-state) after selection and expansion; verify from metadata\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_controls\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Non-targeting controls\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean expression used as baseline for delta predictions\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"decision_support_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support provided. Key risk: distribution shift between training perturbations and held-out perturbations. Model must generalize at the gene level, not memorize perturbation identities. Also consider perturbations targeting genes with no baseline expression in K562; strategy: predict near-control mean with shrinkage.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"samples\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"size\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Total cells likely in the tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands; number of perturbations in training likely in the hundreds to low thousands. Exact counts must be read from the h5ad (n_obs, number of unique perturbation labels). Assumption: sufficient cells per perturbation for stable mean estimation; quantify and flag low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"distribution\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cells per perturbation likely highly variable (many cells for some perturbations, few for others). Control cells may be abundant. Long-tailed distribution of cells per perturbation expected. Quantify cells per perturbation and consider shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"characteristics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562 erythroleukemia cell line\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pooled screen with sgRNA identity\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Counts per 10k + log1p normalized expression\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_variability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cell-to-cell variability in knockdown efficiency, cell cycle state, and clonal heterogeneity. Some perturbations may have strong transcriptional effects; others near-neutral. Assess association between perturbation labels and cell cycle or culture state to identify confounding.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical_variability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Batch effects from pooled screens, library preparation, sequencing depth, and sgRNA capture efficiency. Ambient RNA and doublets may confound perturbation assignment. Check for association between technical covariates (batch, library size) and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"decision_support_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support provided. Recommend verifying batch/replicate columns and cell-level QC metrics before modeling. If raw counts are unavailable, document limitations for QC.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"methods\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Hold-out perturbations by gene identity (unseen-perturbation split)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Evaluate pearson_delta of predicted vs. true change from control\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Evaluate MSE/PCC/R2 on top-20 DE genes per perturbation (DE genes defined as those with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"controls\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Non-targeting controls as baseline\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Permutation tests / control perturbations as negative controls\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"pearson_delta\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"PCC on top-20 DE genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Not available from provided information; must be computed by the evaluator (Gene-Mila).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"High pearson_delta indicates correct direction and magnitude of predicted changes. Top-20 DE gene metrics assess whether the strongest biological effects are captured. Delta computed in log1p space as predicted mean minus control mean.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support inputs. Recommend internal cross-validation on training perturbations (leave-perturbation-out) to estimate generalization before final submission. Define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"methods\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cross-validation by perturbation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Comparison of predicted means to control mean\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sanity checks on gene-wise variance and sparsity\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"controls\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control mean as baseline\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Random perturbation labels as negative control\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. observed mean expression\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Delta correlation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Not available; to be assessed during model development.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Technical validation ensures predictions are not dominated by normalization artifacts or control mean leakage. Dummy submission with control mean should yield zero delta correlation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support provided. Recommend checking that predictions are not simply the control mean. Implement modular validation scripts that mimic evaluator exactly.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"data_properties\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"distribution\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"patterns\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Log1p-normalized counts per 10k; expected overdispersion typical of scRNA-seq\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Gene expression dominated by highly expressed genes (e.g., ribosomal, mitochondrial)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects may be subtle for most genes and strong for a few\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"variability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Biological variability across cells within a perturbation due to cell cycle, clonal heterogeneity, and variable knockdown efficiency.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"outliers\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cells with unusually high mitochondrial or ribosomal content\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Doublets or cells with mixed sgRNA assignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Expression distributions should be inspected per perturbation and per gene; heavy tails and zero-inflation are expected. Acknowledge potential bimodal responses; mean may not capture full distribution but is the required output.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_significance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Strong effects expected for essential genes and known regulators; weak effects for unrelated genes. For perturbations targeting unexpressed genes, predict near-control mean with shrinkage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_implications\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Model should account for overdispersion and sparsity; mean prediction is the target, so cell-level noise should average out.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"patterns\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Library size variation across cells\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch-specific shifts in gene detection\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"sgRNA capture efficiency variation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"variability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Technical variability from sequencing depth, batch, and cell quality.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ambient RNA contamination\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Doublets\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Low-quality cells with high mitochondrial fraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Technical artifacts can bias perturbation means if not controlled. Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cell-level QC filtering with data-driven thresholds\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Include batch as covariate if confounded; avoid aggressive batch correction\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Doublet detection and removal if scores available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Recommend inspecting obs columns for QC metrics (n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito) and batch labels. If raw counts unavailable, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"features\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"types\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"mRNA gene expression\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Perturbation target gene identity\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"relevance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"All genes are potential readouts; top-20 DE genes per perturbation are most relevant for scoring. Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) if allowed, to support feature construction and post-hoc interpretation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"selection\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Use all genes for mean prediction (required output dimension)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"For modeling, consider highly variable genes or genes with detectable expression; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in feature space or model can predict them from gene features\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Gene-level mean expression is the prediction target; feature selection is for model efficiency, not output reduction. Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with pathway enrichment if annotations available.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that selected features capture known perturbation effects (e.g., knockdown of target gene itself); verify pathway annotations cover substantial fraction of genes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_features\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Recommend retaining all genes in the output and using HVGs or all genes for model training depending on method. External annotations allowed only if task permits; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"types\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Gene identifiers (same order as input)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Normalization factors (counts per 10k)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"sgRNA assignments\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"quality\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Gene annotation quality assumed consistent; normalization already applied. Verify gene identifiers are consistent and match evaluator expectations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"selection\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Filter genes with zero expression across all cells (if any) for modeling, but output must include all genes; handle zero-variance genes by excluding from model input but retaining in output\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"interpretation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Technical features should not drive biological predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Verify gene order and normalization match the evaluator's expectations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_technical\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Ensure predictions.npz has perts in the exact order of heldout_perturbations.json and means with shape (n_perts, n_genes), float dtype.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"quality\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Number of cells per perturbation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Knockdown efficiency (target gene expression reduction)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Reproducibility of perturbation effects across cells\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Quality varies by perturbation; some may have few cells and noisy means. Compute and report per-perturbation cell counts, target gene expression reduction, and within-perturbation variance. Prioritize quality issues by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Low cell numbers for some perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Off-target effects\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Weighted mean estimation by cell quality\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Shrinkage of perturbation means toward control for low-cell perturbations with cross-validated shrinkage parameter\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Define minimum cell threshold for including perturbations in training\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare known essential gene knockdowns to expected phenotypes. Check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_quality\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Recommend computing per-perturbation cell counts and target gene knockdown to assess training data quality. Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells to detect imbalances.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"n_genes per cell\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"total counts per cell\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"percent mitochondrial reads (if raw counts available)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"doublet scores (if available)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Standard scRNA-seq QC metrics should be computed from the h5ad. Inspect layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Low-quality cells\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Doublets\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch effects\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high pct_mito using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Remove doublets if scores available\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Include batch as covariate or correct if not confounded\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means for training. Document all QC parameters and filtered cell/gene lists.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"noise\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sources\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cell cycle heterogeneity\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Clonal variation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stochastic gene expression\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Increases variance of perturbation means, especially for low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Estimate by comparing within-perturbation variance to between-perturbation variance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Use robust mean estimation (e.g., trimmed mean)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Model cell-level covariates (cell cycle) if available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_noise\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Biological noise is irreducible but can be averaged out with sufficient cells.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sources\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Library size variation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ambient RNA\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sequencing depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Can bias perturbation means if correlated with perturbation assignment.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assessment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Normalization (already applied as counts per 10k + log1p)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Batch correction or covariate adjustment (prefer covariate if confounding possible)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ambient RNA correction (e.g., SoupX) if raw counts available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare means before and after batch correction; ensure biological effects preserved.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Since normalization is already applied, focus on batch effects and cell quality.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"preprocessing\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"normalization\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"strategies\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"recommendations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Use the provided counts per 10k + log1p normalization as-is for both training and prediction output\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Do not re-normalize if it would break compatibility with the evaluator\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"rationale\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The evaluator expects predictions in the same normalized space as the input; altering normalization would invalidate scoring.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"implementation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Load h5ad, use X as normalized expression. For modeling, optionally standardize features per gene (z-score) but inverse-transform predictions to the original space. All preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline; verify known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Ensure known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"decision_support_normalization\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"No decision support. Strictly preserve the provided normalization for output.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"quality_control\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"procedures\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high mitochondrial fraction using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Remove doublets if scores available\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis correctly identifies the biological context: K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbations with scRNA-seq readout. It emphasizes verification of metadata (cell line, perturbation type, controls, batch) and highlights key biological considerations such as knockdown efficiency, target gene baseline expression, and pathway coverage. The relevance to predicting unseen perturbation effects is well-articulated.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumes K562 and CRISPRi without explicit verification from h5ad metadata; must confirm obs columns.\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not quantify pathway coverage or functional diversity of training perturbations, which is critical for generalization to held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks a concrete plan to identify and handle perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add a metadata verification step: inspect h5ad obs for cell_line, perturbation, control, batch, and QC columns; document actual values.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute pathway enrichment or co-expression module coverage of training perturbations to estimate transferability to held-out targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a rule for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes: predict control mean with strong shrinkage, and flag such cases in validation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify metadata columns match assumptions; compute per-perturbation cell counts, target gene knockdown efficiency, and baseline expression of target genes; check that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty; all assumptions must be explicitly verified against h5ad metadata. Document deviations and ensure biological relevance checks are integrated into the modeling pipeline.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes the need for interpretable predictions, especially given the biological context. It suggests mapping gene identifiers to pathways and using co-expression modules when external annotations are not permitted. However, it lacks a concrete plan for interpreting model outputs at the pathway or module level.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit strategy for interpreting high-dimensional mean predictions beyond top-20 DE genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear whether external pathway annotations are allowed; interpretability may be limited to data-derived features.\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not specify how to handle target genes of held-out perturbations in the output for on-target effect interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Derive co-expression modules from control cells and use module-level summaries to interpret predicted deltas.\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and feature space, even if not expressed in training, to allow on-target effect checks.\\\",\\n          \\\"Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with module enrichment (data-derived) to facilitate biological interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check enrichment of predicted top genes for modules related to the target gene; compare predicted signatures to known K562 CRISPRi phenotypes where available.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For selected validation perturbations, compare predicted signatures to known CRISPRi phenotypes (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown should downregulate translation machinery).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; interpretability must rely on provided data. Recommend deriving modules from control expression and documenting all interpretability steps.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly identifies key technical quality issues: variable cell numbers per perturbation, batch effects, ambient RNA, doublets, and normalization. It recommends data-driven QC thresholds and checking for confounding between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumes raw counts or QC metrics may be available; must verify what is present in h5ad (X, layers, obs).\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not specify how to handle potential confounding between batch and perturbation labels if present.\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks a concrete plan for doublet detection if scores are not provided.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect h5ad for raw counts (layers) and QC metrics (obs columns); if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Test association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, include batch as covariate rather than aggressive correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"If doublet scores are unavailable, consider using a simple doublet detection method on highly variable genes or flag as a limitation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells; check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Implement automated checks for gene order, normalization, and output format; verify that predictions are not simply the control mean.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means and documenting all QC parameters and filtered cell/gene lists.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis mentions deterministic seeds, pinned software versions, and saved intermediate artifacts, but does not provide a detailed reproducibility protocol. Reproducibility is critical for fair evaluation and iterative improvement.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit list of software versions or environment specification.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how random seeds are set for all stochastic steps (e.g., model initialization, data splitting).\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not specify archiving of intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Create a reproducible environment (e.g., conda env or Docker) with pinned versions of anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and any deep learning libraries.\\\",\\n          \\\"Set and record random seeds for all stochastic steps; use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with fixed solver).\\\",\\n          \\\"Archive intermediate outputs (QC-filtered h5ad, control mean vector, perturbation means, model coefficients, CV fold assignments) for audit.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Implement automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json, and verify dtype.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend a reproducibility checklist and version-controlled code repository.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly recommends using the provided counts per 10k + log1p normalization as-is and avoiding re-normalization that would break evaluator compatibility. It also suggests fitting preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Does not specify how to handle genes with zero expression across all cells: filter for modeling but retain in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how to treat low-expression genes for modeling while still predicting all genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit recommendation for handling batch effects in preprocessing if confounding is present.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Explicitly state: filter zero-variance genes for model input but retain all genes in output; handle zero-variance genes by predicting control mean.\\\",\\n          \\\"Recommend including batch as a covariate in models if batch is associated with perturbation labels, rather than aggressive batch correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify that HVG selection and scaling must be fit on training data only and applied to validation/test data.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline; verify known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad, use X as normalized expression. For modeling, optionally standardize features per gene (z-score) but inverse-transform predictions to the original space. All preprocessing steps fit on training data only.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; strictly preserve the provided normalization for output and document all preprocessing choices.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis includes validation steps for preprocessing, such as checking control mean and housekeeping gene stability, but lacks a systematic validation protocol for QC filtering and batch handling.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No quantitative threshold for acceptable stability of perturbation means under subsampling.\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not specify how to validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how to validate batch effect handling.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define quantitative thresholds: e.g., correlation of perturbation means across subsamples > 0.9; compare means before and after QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare distribution of QC metrics between control and perturbed cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"If batch is included as covariate, compare model performance with and without batch to justify inclusion.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Implement a preprocessing validation script that computes control mean, checks housekeeping gene stability, and compares perturbation means before and after QC.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Run validation on a subset of perturbations with sufficient cells; document all validation results and thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend modular validation scripts that mimic evaluator exactly.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The analysis provides a thorough evaluation of biological and technical quality metrics, including cell numbers per perturbation, knockdown efficiency, QC metrics, and noise sources. It recommends data-driven thresholds and mitigation strategies.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Does not prioritize quality issues by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks a concrete plan for handling low-cell perturbations beyond shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how to assess confounding with cell cycle or copy-number alterations if metadata is limited.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Prioritize quality issues: low cell numbers and batch confounding are most critical; knockdown efficiency and doublets are next.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a minimum cell threshold for including perturbations in training; for low-cell perturbations, use shrinkage toward control with cross-validated shrinkage parameter.\\\",\\n          \\\"If cell cycle or copy-number scores are available in obs, test association with perturbation labels; otherwise acknowledge as a limitation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compute per-perturbation cell counts, target gene knockdown, and within-perturbation variance; report these as part of quality assessment.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement a quality assessment module that outputs a table of metrics per perturbation and flags low-quality perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_assessment\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend computing per-perturbation cell counts and target gene knockdown to assess training data quality.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The analysis suggests validation via subsampling stability and comparison of QC metrics, but lacks a systematic validation framework for quality assessment.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No quantitative criteria for acceptable quality (e.g., minimum cells per perturbation, minimum knockdown efficiency).\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not specify how to validate that quality filtering improves model performance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how to validate noise mitigation strategies.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define quantitative quality criteria: e.g., minimum 20 cells per perturbation for stable mean estimation; minimum 20% knockdown efficiency for on-target perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate that quality filtering improves cross-validation performance; compare models with and without QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate noise mitigation by comparing model performance under noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Implement a validation script that computes quality metrics and compares model performance across QC thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Run cross-valid