ut, batch effects).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Implement a validation script that computes quality metrics and compares model performance across QC thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Run cross-validation with different QC thresholds and select the threshold that maximizes validation performance without removing entire perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend documenting all QC parameters and validating their impact on model performance.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"problem\\\": {\\n    \\\"definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition clearly scopes the task: predict mean log1p CP10K expression for unseen CRISPRi perturbations in K562, using only provided data. It articulates biological constraints, evaluation criteria, and limitations. The scope is appropriate and well-aligned with the task.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations without a concrete plan to verify across batches.\\\",\\n          \\\"Does not quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations, which is essential for assessing feasibility of generalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks explicit handling of perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression beyond shrinkage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add a step to compare control mean estimates across batches to verify representativeness.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway and essential gene coverage of training perturbations to estimate transferability.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a rule for zero-baseline target genes: predict near-control mean with strong shrinkage and flag in validation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training; check control mean stability across batches; compute pathway coverage.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Select known essential genes in K562 and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_scope\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty; scope must be verified against h5ad metadata and task rules. Document any deviations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clarity\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition is clear and well-structured, with formal input-output mapping, biological constraints, and evaluation criteria. It clearly states the unseen-perturbation setting and the required output format.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Some assumptions (e.g., cell line, perturbation type) are stated but not yet verified from metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"The definition of top-20 DE genes relies on true held-out data, which is only available to the evaluator; this is clear but should be emphasized in internal validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"The scope of external annotations (e.g., pathway databases) is ambiguous; clarity is needed on whether they are permitted.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add a metadata verification step at the beginning of the pipeline to confirm assumptions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Clarify that internal validation should mimic the evaluator's definition of top-20 DE genes using held-out training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Explicitly state whether external annotations are allowed; if not, derive features from provided data only.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Document metadata verification results and any deviations from assumptions; ensure internal validation metrics match evaluator definitions.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that biological constraints (e.g., non-negativity, plausible expression ranges) are enforced in predictions.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_clarity\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; clarity must be achieved through explicit documentation of assumptions and verification steps.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feasibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem is feasible with simple baselines, but generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging. The analysis acknowledges computational and data limitations and suggests practical approaches (shrinkage, low-rank models).\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Limited number of perturbations may restrict model complexity; pathway coverage may be insufficient for some held-out targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational resources (32 GB RAM) may be insufficient for very large matrices or deep models; need to verify dataset size.\\\",\\n          \\\"No contingency plan if simple models fail to beat control baseline.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Quantify dataset size (n_cells, n_genes, n_perturbations) early to plan computational resources.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a fallback strategy: if pearson_delta \\u2264 0, use control mean with shrinkage; establish minimum acceptable performance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Consider dimensionality reduction (PCA) early to reduce memory and compute.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Run pilot experiments with simple baselines to assess feasibility and runtime; measure memory usage.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure that feasibility constraints do not compromise biological validity; e.g., shrinkage should not eliminate true strong effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_feasibility\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; feasibility must be assessed through pilot experiments and resource checks.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"challenges\\\": {\\n      \\\"identification\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis identifies key biological and technical challenges: distribution shift to unseen perturbations, weak effects, noisy means, batch effects, and high-dimensional output. The challenges are comprehensive and well-prioritized.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Does not explicitly prioritize challenges by expected impact on evaluation metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks a plan to quantify the extent of distribution shift (e.g., pathway dissimilarity between training and held-out perturbations).\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear how to handle perturbations with no close functional analog in training.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Prioritize challenges: distribution shift to unseen perturbations and weak effects are most critical; batch effects and noise are next.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway dissimilarity between training and held-out perturbations to estimate difficulty.\\\",\\n          \\\"For functionally isolated perturbations, predict near-control mean with strong shrinkage and flag as low-confidence.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Use internal leave-perturbation-out validation stratified by pathway to assess generalization across functional categories.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that mitigations preserve known biological effects; e.g., shrinkage should not eliminate strong on-target knockdown signals.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_challenges\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; challenge identification must be validated through pilot experiments.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis proposes reasonable mitigation strategies: shrinkage, low-rank models, pathway-level modules, and robust control baseline estimation. However, it lacks specificity on how to tune shrinkage and handle confounding.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Shrinkage parameter tuning is mentioned but not detailed; need a cross-validation strategy over perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effect mitigation is vague; unclear when to include batch as covariate vs. correct.\\\",\\n          \\\"No concrete plan for handling perturbations targeting unexpressed genes beyond shrinkage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify a cross-validation procedure to tune shrinkage parameter (e.g., grid search over blending weights with leave-perturbation-out CV).\\\",\\n          \\\"Test association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, include batch as covariate in models; avoid aggressive batch correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"For unexpressed target genes, predict control mean with shrinkage weight near 1; validate on training perturbations with low target expression.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Evaluate mitigation strategies via cross-validation; compare model performance with and without each mitigation.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure mitigations do not introduce biologically implausible effects; check that known strong effects are preserved.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_mitigation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; mitigation strategies must be validated empirically.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"questions\\\": {\\n      \\\"formulation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis does not explicitly formulate research questions or hypotheses, but the problem definition implies key questions: how well can models generalize to unseen perturbations? What features enable transfer? The formulation could be more explicit.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit research questions or hypotheses are stated.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear which aspects of the problem will be investigated (e.g., effect of pathway similarity, target expression).\\\",\\n          \\\"Lacks a structured plan for hypothesis testing.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Formulate exp