      "content": "You are refining the Dataset Analysis for a scientific task analysis.\nApply the reviewer comments to improve accuracy, completeness, and implementation usefulness.\n\nSECTION TO REFINE:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": {\\n    \\\"experimental_design\\\": {\\n      \\\"setup\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_conditions\\\": [\\n          \\\"K562 cells carrying CRISPRi perturbations (gene knockdown via dCas9-KRAB)\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cells: non-targeting controls and possibly safe-harbor controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"experimental_protocol\\\": \\\"Pooled CRISPRi screen in K562 cells with single-cell RNA-seq readout; perturbations identified by sgRNA capture; expression quantified as counts per 10k followed by log1p normalization. Assumption: cell line and perturbation type are as stated in task description; must be verified from h5ad metadata (obs columns for cell line, perturbation, control labels).\\\",\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting sgRNA controls\\\",\\n          \\\"Safe-harbor / intergenic controls (if present)\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cells used to compute delta expression for scoring\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"replicates\\\": \\\"Likely multiple cells per perturbation (pooled screen); true biological replicates may be limited to one or few batches. Replicate structure must be verified from metadata (e.g., batch, lane, replicate columns). Assumption: if multiple batches exist, batch is not fully confounded with perturbation; this must be checked.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbations are completely unseen; no direct biological validation available within the training file. Validation relies on known biology (e.g., knockdown of essential genes) and consistency of predicted effects with known pathways. Plan: select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check expected expression changes; compare predicted top genes for validation perturbations to literature signatures.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty; analysis is based solely on task description and dataset metadata. All assumptions must be explicitly verified against the actual h5ad metadata (obs columns for perturbation, control, batch, cell cycle, QC metrics). Document any deviations from assumed design.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"perturbation\\\": {\\n        \\\"design\\\": \\\"CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) gene knockdown, pooled format. Each perturbation targets one gene; cells with the same sgRNA are grouped as a perturbation. Held-out perturbations have zero cells in the training file (unseen-perturbation setting). Assumption: perturbation labels in h5ad correspond to target genes; verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training.\\\",\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown\\\",\\n          \\\"Non-targeting control\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"doses\\\": [\\n          \\\"Single constitutive knockdown (no dose variation expected)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"timepoints\\\": [\\n          \\\"Likely a single endpoint (steady-state) after selection and expansion; verify from metadata\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting controls\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean expression used as baseline for delta predictions\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Key risk: distribution shift between training perturbations and held-out perturbations. Model must generalize at the gene level, not memorize perturbation identities. Also consider perturbations targeting genes with no baseline expression in K562; strategy: predict near-control mean with strong shrinkage.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"samples\\\": {\\n      \\\"size\\\": \\\"Total cells likely in the tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands; number of perturbations in training likely in the hundreds to low thousands. Exact counts must be read from the h5ad (n_obs, number of unique perturbation labels). Assumption: sufficient cells per perturbation for stable mean estimation; quantify and flag low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"distribution\\\": \\\"Cells per perturbation likely highly variable (many cells for some perturbations, few for others). Control cells may be abundant. Long-tailed distribution of cells per perturbation expected. Quantify cells per perturbation and consider shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n      \\\"characteristics\\\": [\\n        \\\"K562 erythroleukemia cell line\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown\\\",\\n        \\\"Pooled screen with sgRNA identity\\\",\\n        \\\"Counts per 10k + log1p normalized expression\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_variability\\\": \\\"Cell-to-cell variability in knockdown efficiency, cell cycle state, and clonal heterogeneity. Some perturbations may have strong transcriptional effects; others near-neutral. Assess association between perturbation labels and cell cycle or culture state to identify confounding.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_variability\\\": \\\"Batch effects from pooled screens, library preparation, sequencing depth, and sgRNA capture efficiency. Ambient RNA and doublets may confound perturbation assignment. Check for association between technical covariates (batch, library size) and perturbation labels.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_requirements\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Recommend verifying batch/replicate columns and cell-level QC metrics before modeling. If raw counts are unavailable, document limitations for QC.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Hold-out perturbations by gene identity (unseen-perturbation split)\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate pearson_delta of predicted vs. true change from control\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate MSE/PCC/R2 on top-20 DE genes per perturbation (DE genes defined as those with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\",\\n          \\\"Internal leave-perturbation-out cross-validation stratified by pathway or target expression to mimic evaluator's top-20 DE gene definition\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting controls as baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Permutation tests / control perturbations as negative controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"PCC on top-20 DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not available from provided information; must be computed by the evaluator (Gene-Mila).\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"High pearson_delta indicates correct direction and magnitude of predicted changes. Top-20 DE gene metrics assess whether the strongest biological effects are captured. Delta computed in log1p space as predicted mean minus control mean.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support inputs. Recommend internal cross-validation on training perturbations (leave-perturbation-out) to estimate generalization before final submission. Define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation by perturbation\\\",\\n          \\\"Comparison of predicted means to control mean\\\",\\n          \\\"Sanity checks on gene-wise variance and sparsity\\\",\\n          \\\"Automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json, verify dtype\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean as baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Random perturbation labels as negative control\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. observed mean expression\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"Delta correlation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not available; to be assessed during model development.\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical validation ensures predictions are not dominated by normalization artifacts or control mean leakage. Dummy submission with control mean should yield zero delta correlation.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided. Recommend checking that predictions are not simply the control mean. Implement modular validation scripts that mimic evaluator exactly.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"data_properties\\\": {\\n      \\\"distribution\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n            \\\"Log1p-normalized counts per 10k; expected overdispersion typical of scRNA-seq\\\",\\n            \\\"Gene expression dominated by highly expressed genes (e.g., ribosomal, mitochondrial)\\\",\\n            \\\"Perturbation effects may be subtle for most genes and strong for a few\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Biological variability across cells within a perturbation due to cell cycle, clonal heterogeneity, and variable knockdown efficiency.\\\",\\n          \\\"outliers\\\": [\\n            \\\"Cells with unusually high mitochondrial or ribosomal content\\\",\\n            \\\"Doublets or cells with mixed sgRNA assignment\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Expression distributions should be inspected per perturbation and per gene; heavy tails and zero-inflation are expected. Acknowledge potential bimodal responses; mean may not capture full distribution but is the required output.\\\",\\n          \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Strong effects expected for essential genes and known regulators; weak effects for unrelated genes. For perturbations targeting unexpressed genes, predict near-control mean with shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_implications\\\": \\\"No decision support. Model should account for overdispersion and sparsity; mean prediction is the target, so cell-level noise should average out.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n            \\\"Library size variation across cells\\\",\\n            \\\"Batch-specific shifts in gene detection\\\",\\n            \\\"sgRNA capture efficiency variation\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Technical variability from sequencing depth, batch, and cell quality.\\\",\\n          \\\"artifacts\\\": [\\n            \\\"Ambient RNA contamination\\\",\\n            \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n            \\\"Low-quality cells with high mitochondrial fraction\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical artifacts can bias perturbation means if not controlled. Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Cell-level QC filtering with data-driven thresholds\\\",\\n            \\\"Include batch as covariate if confounded; avoid aggressive batch correction\\\",\\n            \\\"Doublet detection and removal if scores available; otherwise flag as limitation\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend inspecting obs columns for QC metrics (n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito) and batch labels. If raw counts unavailable, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"features\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"types\\\": [\\n            \\\"mRNA gene expression\\\",\\n            \\\"Perturbation target gene identity\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"All genes are potential readouts; top-20 DE genes per perturbation are most relevant for scoring. Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) if allowed, to support feature construction and post-hoc interpretation. If external annotations are not permitted, derive co-expression modules from control cells to interpret predicted deltas.\\\",\\n          \\\"selection\\\": [\\n            \\\"Use all genes for mean prediction (required output dimension)\\\",\\n            \\\"For modeling, consider highly variable genes or genes with detectable expression; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in feature space or model can predict them from gene features\\\",\\n            \\\"Filter zero-variance genes for model input but retain all genes in output; predict control mean for zero-variance genes\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Gene-level mean expression is the prediction target; feature selection is for model efficiency, not output reduction. Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with module enrichment (data-derived) if annotations unavailable.\\\",\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that selected features capture known perturbation effects (e.g., knockdown of target gene itself); verify pathway annotations cover substantial fraction of genes; for interpretability, check enrichment of predicted top genes for modules related to the target gene.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend retaining all genes in the output and using HVGs or all genes for model training depending on method. External annotations allowed only if task permits; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"types\\\": [\\n            \\\"Gene identifiers (same order as input)\\\",\\n            \\\"Normalization factors (counts per 10k)\\\",\\n            \\\"sgRNA assignments\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Gene annotation quality assumed consistent; normalization already applied. Verify gene identifiers are consistent and match evaluator expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"selection\\\": [\\n            \\\"Filter genes with zero expression across all cells (if any) for modeling, but output must include all genes; handle zero-variance genes by excluding from model input but retaining in output\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical features should not drive biological predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify gene order and normalization match the evaluator's expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_technical\\\": \\\"No decision support. Ensure predictions.npz has perts in the exact order of heldout_perturbations.json and means with shape (n_perts, n_genes), float dtype.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"Number of cells per perturbation\\\",\\n            \\\"Knockdown efficiency (target gene expression reduction)\\\",\\n            \\\"Reproducibility of perturbation effects across cells\\\",\\n            \\\"Baseline expression of target genes\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Quality varies by perturbation; some may have few cells and noisy means. Compute and report per-perturbation cell counts, target gene expression reduction, and within-perturbation variance. Prioritize quality issues by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization: low cell numbers and batch confounding are most critical; knockdown efficiency and doublets are next.\\\",\\n          \\\"issues\\\": [\\n            \\\"Low cell numbers for some perturbations\\\",\\n            \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n            \\\"Off-target effects\\\",\\n            \\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Weighted mean estimation by cell quality\\\",\\n            \\\"Shrinkage of perturbation means toward control for low-cell perturbations with cross-validated shrinkage parameter\\\",\\n            \\\"Define minimum cell threshold (e.g., 20 cells) for including perturbations in training\\\",\\n            \\\"For perturbations targeting unexpressed genes, predict control mean with strong shrinkage (weight near 1) and flag in validation\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare known essential gene knockdowns to expected phenotypes. Check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9). Compute per-perturbation cell counts, target gene knockdown, and within-perturbation variance; report these as part of quality assessment.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend computing per-perturbation cell counts and target gene knockdown to assess training data quality. Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells to detect imbalances. Implement a quality assessment module that outputs a table of metrics per perturbation and flags low-quality perturbations.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"n_genes per cell\\\",\\n            \\\"total counts per cell\\\",\\n            \\\"percent mitochondrial reads (if raw counts available)\\\",\\n            \\\"doublet scores (if available)\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Standard scRNA-seq QC metrics should be computed from the h5ad. Inspect layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"issues\\\": [\\n            \\\"Low-quality cells\\\",\\n            \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n            \\\"Batch effects\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high pct_mito using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\\\",\\n            \\\"Remove doublets if scores available; otherwise consider a simple doublet detection method on highly variable genes or flag as a limitation\\\",\\n            \\\"Include batch as covariate if confounded; avoid aggressive batch correction\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable. Validate that quality filtering improves cross-validation performance; compare models with and without QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means for training. Document all QC parameters and filtered cell/gene lists. Run cross-validation with different QC thresholds and select the threshold that maximizes validation performance without removing entire perturbations.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"noise\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"sources\\\": [\\n            \\\"Cell cycle heterogeneity\\\",\\n            \\\"Clonal variation\\\",\\n            \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n            \\\"Stochastic gene expression\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Increases variance of perturbation means, especially for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Estimate by comparing within-perturbation variance to between-perturbation variance. If cell cycle or copy-number scores are available in obs, test association with perturbation labels; otherwise acknowledge as a limitation.\\\",\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Use robust mean estimation (e.g., trimmed mean)\\\",\\n            \\\"Model cell-level covariates (cell cycle) if available\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation on training perturbations. Validate noise mitigation by comparing model performance under noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects).\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_noise\\\": \\\"No decision support. Biological noise is irreducible but can be averaged out with sufficient cells.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"sources\\\": [\\n            \\\"Library size variation\\\",\\n            \\\"Batch effects\\\",\\n            \\\"Ambient RNA\\\",\\n            \\\"Sequencing depth\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Can bias perturbation means if correlated with perturbation assignment.\\\",\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Check for association between technical covariates and perturbation labels. Test association between batch and perturbation labels; if confounded, include batch as covariate rather than aggressive correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Normalization (already applied as counts per 10k + log1p)\\\",\\n            \\\"Batch correction or covariate adjustment (prefer covariate if confounding possible)\\\",\\n            \\\"Ambient RNA correction (e.g., SoupX) if raw counts available\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare means before and after batch correction; ensure biological effects preserved. If batch is included as covariate, compare model performance with and without batch to justify inclusion.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\": \\\"No decision support. Since normalization is already applied, focus on batch effects and cell quality.\\\"\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"normalization\\\": {\\n        \\\"strategies\\\": {\\n          \\\"recommendations\\\": [\\n            \\\"Use the provided counts per 10k + log1p normalization as-is for both training and prediction output\\\",\\n            \\\"Do not re-normalize if it would break compatibility with the evaluator\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The evaluator expects predictions in the same normalized space as the input; altering normalization would invalidate scoring.\\\",\\n          \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad, use X as normalized expression. For modeling, optionally standardize features per gene (z-score) but inverse-transform predictions to the original space. All preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) fit on training data only to avoid leakage. Explicitly filter zero-variance genes for model input but retain all genes in output; handle zero-variance genes by predicting control mean. Include batch as a covariate in models if batch is associated with perturbation labels, rather than aggressive batch correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline; verify known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations. Define quantitative thresholds: e.g., correlation of perturbation means across subsamples > 0.9; compare means before and after QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure known housekeeping genes have stable expression across perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_normalization\\\": \\\"No decision support. Strictly preserve the provided normalization for output. Document all preprocessing choices.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"quality_control\\\": {\\n          \\\"procedures\\\": [\\n            \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high mitochondrial fraction using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD)\\\",\\n            \\\"Remove doublets if scores available; otherwise consider a simple doublet detection method on highly variable genes or flag as a limitation\\\",\\n            \\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare distribution of QC metrics between control and perturbed cells\\\",\\n            \\\"Compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers and control baseline are stable\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Run\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis correctly identifies the biological context (K562 CRISPRi screen) and emphasizes the need to verify assumptions against h5ad metadata. The focus on unseen-perturbation generalization and biological validation via known essential genes is appropriate.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Assumptions about cell line, perturbation type, and control labels are not yet verified from metadata, which could lead to misalignment with the actual data.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological validation plan lacks specific quantitative thresholds and named gene sets (e.g., which ribosomal genes) for essential gene checks.\\\",\\n          \\\"No explicit consideration of K562-specific copy-number alterations and their impact on expression and perturbation effects.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add a mandatory metadata verification step to confirm cell line, perturbation type, control labels, and batch structure before any modeling.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify concrete gene sets (e.g., ribosomal protein genes RPS/RPL, proteasome subunits) and expected direction of change for biological validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Include a plan to assess copy-number effects, e.g., by checking expression of genes on commonly amplified regions in K562 and flagging potential confounders.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify that control cells are representative across batches by comparing control mean expression per batch (e.g., Pearson correlation > 0.9). Confirm that known housekeeping genes show stable expression across perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Select at least 5 known essential genes in K562 (e.g., RPS3, RPL13, PSMA1) and check that their knockdown reduces expression of translation/proteasome machinery. Use literature-based signatures to validate predicted top genes for these perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support inputs were empty; the analysis appropriately relies on task description and metadata. Recommend documenting all assumptions and their verification status in a decision log to support future model selection and risk assessment.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes the importance of interpretability, focusing on gene-level mean expression and delta from control. It suggests using co-expression modules when external annotations are not permitted, which is a practical approach.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No concrete plan for deriving and validating co-expression modules from control cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Interpretation of predicted deltas for top genes lacks a standardized enrichment method when external annotations are unavailable.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address how to handle genes with zero variance in the output, which may complicate interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define a procedure to derive co-expression modules (e.g., hierarchical clustering or WGCNA on control cells) and use them for enrichment of predicted top genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pre-specify that zero-variance genes will be predicted as control mean and flagged in interpretation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Include a step to map predicted top genes to modules and report module-level enrichment scores (e.g., hypergeometric test) for each perturbation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that derived co-expression modules are biologically coherent (e.g., enrichment for known pathways if annotations allowed) and stable across control cell subsamples.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For a subset of training perturbations with known biology, verify that predicted top genes are enriched in modules related to the target gene's function (e.g., ribosomal module for ribosomal gene knockdown).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend that interpretability outputs (module enrichment, top gene lists) be included in model comparison to inform selection of models that are both accurate and biologically plausible.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis outlines appropriate technical quality metrics (n_genes, total_counts, pct_mito) and mitigation strategies (QC filtering, doublet detection). However, it relies on assumptions about data availability (e.g., raw counts) that must be verified.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable if only normalized X is present.\\\",\\n          \\\"No specific thresholds for QC filtering are provided; data-driven thresholds are mentioned but not defined.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch confounding with perturbation labels is not quantitatively assessed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect h5ad layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define QC thresholds explicitly (e.g., filter cells with n_genes < 200 or pct_mito > 20% if computable) and justify based on data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify association between batch and perturbation labels (e.g., chi-square test or Cram\\u00e9r's V) to decide whether to include batch as covariate.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells (e.g., Mann-Whitney U test) to detect imbalances. Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations (minimum 10 cells retained per perturbation).\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Validate that QC filtering improves cross-validation performance by comparing models with and without QC. Ensure that key markers (e.g., GATA1, HBB in K562) remain detectable after filtering.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend creating a QC report with per-perturbation cell counts and flagging low-quality perturbations. Use this report to decide on inclusion/exclusion or shrinkage for low-cell perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis mentions deterministic seeds and pinned software versions but lacks a detailed reproducibility plan. The need for automated format checks is correctly identified.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specification of how to save and version intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, model coefficients).\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan for archiving cross-validation folds and random seeds.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks) is not fully addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Implement a reproducibility checklist: fixed seeds for all stochastic steps, pinned package versions (e.g., via requirements.txt), saved CV folds, and archived intermediate outputs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with 'svd' solver) and document any non-deterministic components.\\\",\\n          \\\"Store all artifacts in a versioned directory structure with a manifest file.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions (e.g., max absolute difference < 1e-6). Compare outputs across different seeds to assess stability (e.g., Pearson correlation > 0.95).\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Automate format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json, verify dtype is float.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend including reproducibility metrics (e.g., seed stability) in model comparison to favor models with stable performance.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing recommendations correctly emphasize preserving the provided normalization and fitting all transformations on training data only. The plan to handle zero-variance genes is appropriate.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific guidance on HVG selection if used for modeling; risk of leakage if not fit on training only.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction is mentioned but the preference for covariate adjustment over aggressive correction is not operationalized.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan for handling genes with zero expression across all cells in the output.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify that HVG selection, scaling, and any feature selection must be fit on training perturbations only and applied to held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a rule for batch handling: if batch is associated with perturbation labels (e.g., Cram\\u00e9r's V > 0.3), include batch as a covariate in models; otherwise, no correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"For zero-variance genes, predict control mean and document this in the output metadata.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify that control mean computed from X matches expected baseline (e.g., compare to known housekeeping gene expression). Check that HVG selection does not exclude target genes of held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement preprocessing as a scikit-learn Pipeline or custom transformer that is fit on training data and applied to held-out perturbations. Save the fitted transformer for reproducibility.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend that preprocessing choices be evaluated via cross-validation and documented in a decision log to inform model selection.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The validation plan for preprocessing is reasonable but lacks quantitative thresholds for key checks (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples).\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific threshold for mean stability across subsamples.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan to validate that QC filtering does not disproportionately remove cells with strong perturbation effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"No check for normalization artifacts (e.g., library size correlation with perturbation labels).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Set quantitative thresholds: e.g., correlation of perturbation means across subsamples > 0.9; library size difference between control and perturbed cells < 10%.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare distribution of QC metrics between control and perturbed cells; flag if significant differences (e.g., p < 0.05).\\\",\\n          \\\"Check that normalization (CP10K + log1p) does not introduce artifacts by comparing to raw counts if available.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Perform subsampling stability analysis: randomly split cells per perturbation into two halves, compute means, and require Pearson correlation > 0.9 for perturbations with >20 cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement validation checks as automated scripts that output a report with pass/fail flags for each criterion.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No decision support provided; recommend using these validation results to decide on QC thresholds and preprocessing steps, and