earson correlation, and R2 restricted to the top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation, defined from true held-out data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological plausibility: predicted effects should align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi knockdown direction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Reproducibility: predictions must be generated from a deterministic or seeded pipeline using only provided data, with fixed seeds, pinned software versions, saved cross-validation folds, and archived intermediate artifacts.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"success_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"pearson_delta above a baseline of zero-effect (control-only) prediction and above simple linear baselines.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"MSE lower than control-only baseline and lower than naive gene-mean baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC and R2 significantly above zero-effect baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Consistent improvement across perturbations rather than driven by a few outliers.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological validation: predicted top genes for known pathways show expected knockdown signatures where prior knowledge exists (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown downregulates translation machinery).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"key_variables\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation target gene identity and its functional role in K562.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Baseline expression of target gene and pathway members; flag targets with zero/near-zero baseline expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships and co-expression modules derived from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown efficiency and off-target effects (quantify for training perturbations; treat as unobserved for held-out).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cell cycle, stress, and culture-state variation in K562 (assess via QC metrics and cell cycle scores if available).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Copy-number alterations and karyotype of K562 affecting expression (acknowledge as confounder; assess via expression patterns if possible).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Single-cell library size and dropout.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch effects across perturbation plates or runs; test association with perturbation labels before correction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Guide RNA representation and multiplicity of infection.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Normalization (CP10K, log1p) and gene filtering; verify normalization is already applied in h5ad.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbation list and gene ordering; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in output and feature space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Train/test split by perturbation (unseen-perturbation setting).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"relationships\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation target gene expression is reduced in its own knockdown cells (on-target effect); quantify for training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Knockdown of a regulator propagates to downstream targets and pathways.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Genes with similar functions or co-expression tend to have correlated perturbation responses.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean expression is the baseline from which deltas are computed.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may share pathway membership with training perturbations, enabling transfer; quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Only cellforge_input.h5ad may be used for training.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No held-out perturbation cells are available for training or fine-tuning.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Output must match gene order and normalization exactly; implement automated format checks (keys 'perts' and 'means', shapes, dtype).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions are means, not single-cell distributions; bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Computational resources should be reasonable for a single-machine pipeline (at least 32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hold out a subset of training perturbations to simulate unseen-perturbation prediction; define folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Compare against control-only and gene-mean baselines.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Report pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics on internal validation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Check biological coherence of predicted top genes for validation perturbations; compare to known essential gene knockdowns (e.g., ribosomal genes).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Ensure deterministic seeds and documented preprocessing; re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) are fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Validate that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls; compare perturbation means before and after QC.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Metadata verification: inspect h5ad obs for cell_line, perturbation, control, batch, and QC columns; document actual values and confirm assumptions (K562, CRISPRi, control labels).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Quantify pathway/co-expression module coverage of training perturbations to estimate transferability to held-out targets.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Define and validate rule for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes: predict control mean with strong shrinkage and flag in validation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"scope\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbations; prediction of mean transcriptional response to unseen gene knockdowns; focus on gene-level mean expression and delta from control. Scope assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations; this must be checked across batches. Perturbations targeting unexpressed genes should be predicted near control mean with strong shrinkage. Bimodal responses are acknowledged as a limitation of mean-based evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Single-cell RNA-seq matrix processing, control baseline estimation, feature construction from gene identity and expression, regression/transfer learning from training perturbations to held-out perturbations, and evaluation with Gene-Mila metrics. Feature representation questions focus on features derivable from provided data (co-expression, target expression) unless external annotations are permitted.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot model single-cell heterogeneity or cell-state-specific responses.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Limited ability to predict strong effects for perturbations with no pathway overlap with training perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi efficiency and off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562-specific copy-number and culture effects may confound generalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Evaluation is on means, so sparse or bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Assumption that perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function may not hold for all genes (e.g., off-target effects).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells in training are representative of the control baseline for held-out perturbations; verify by comparing control means across batches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene identifiers are consistent and sufficient to derive functional features; verify coverage of pathway annotations if used.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function and pathway membership.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean expression is a meaningful summary of perturbation response.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Training perturbations cover enough functional diversity to allow transfer to held-out perturbations; quantify pathway and essential gene coverage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"If external pathway annotations are used, they are permitted by task rules; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use internal held-out perturbations to verify that predicted deltas recover known pathway signatures, that on-target knockdown is reflected for target genes when possible, and that top predicted genes are enriched for relevant biological processes. Select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., ribosomal genes) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes. Confirm predicted effects for selected perturbations with independent qPCR or targeted RNA-seq where feasible.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"key_challenges\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no close functional analog in training.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are often weak and variable, making deltas hard to predict.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562 has complex copy-number alterations that distort expression and perturbation response.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pathway redundancy and feedback can mask or reverse expected effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects may depend on cell state not captured in mean expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Poor generalization to functionally isolated perturbations and underestimation of true effects, reducing pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics. The most critical challenges are distribution shift to unseen perturbations, weak effects, and noisy means.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use gene functional embeddings and pathway annotations to relate held-out targets to training perturbations only if permitted; otherwise derive co-expression modules from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Shrink predictions toward control to avoid spurious large effects; tune shrinkage parameter by cross-validation over perturbations (e.g., ridge regularization or explicit blending with control mean).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model perturbation effects as low-rank or pathway-level modules rather than independent gene effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Incorporate target gene baseline expression and network centrality as features; predict near control mean with strong shrinkage for targets with zero baseline expression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use robust control baseline estimation across batches; prefer including batch as covariate over batch correction when confounding is possible.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Internal leave-perturbation-out validation stratified by pathway and target expression to assess generalization across functional categories. Use pilot experiments to confirm which challenges actually degrade performance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Predictions should respect directionality of known regulatory relationships and avoid biologically implausible large effects for genes not connected to the target. Check that mitigations preserve known biological effects.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"High-dimensional output (thousands of genes) with limited number of perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Sparse single-cell data and dropout affecting mean estimates.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch effects between perturbation groups and controls; test association with perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Need to match exact gene order and normalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Overfitting to training perturbations, noisy control baseline, and poor calibration of predicted means.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Aggregate to pseudo-bulk means per perturbation to reduce noise.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use low-rank regression, factor models, or kernel methods on gene features.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Apply strong regularization and cross-validation over perturbations; define and save folds by perturbation ID.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Estimate control mean robustly using all control cells and batch covariates; compare control mean estimates across batches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Validate output shape, gene order, and normalization against input; implement automated format checks.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Specify fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps, record software versions, use deterministic solvers where possible, and save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Cross-validation by perturbation with held-out metric computation and sanity checks on output dimensions and value ranges. Re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions; compare outputs across seeds to assess stability.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"implementation_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and optionally torch; deterministic seeds; pinned software versions; memory sufficient for the full matrix and model (at least 32 GB RAM).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"data_quality\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Variable cell numbers per perturbation; quantify and flag low-cell perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-expression genes with high dropout.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential guide-level or batch confounding; test association between batch and perturbation labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells may differ in quality from perturbed cells; compare QC metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Possible doublets or low-quality cells.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unclear whether raw counts are available; if only normalized X is present, mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Noisy perturbation means and biased control baseline, leading to inaccurate delta predictions. Quality issues should be prioritized by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Filter low-quality cells and genes with very low detection using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD); verify that filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use pseudo-bulk averaging and variance stabilization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Include batch/plate covariates if available and confounding is present.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Robustly estimate control mean with trimming or median-based approaches.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Downsample or weight perturbations to avoid domination by large groups.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Define a 