der highly variable genes or genes with detectable expression; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are included in feature space or model can predict them from gene features\\\",\\n            \\\"Filter zero-variance genes for model input but retain all genes in output; predict control mean for zero-variance genes and document this in output metadata\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Gene-level mean expression is the prediction target; feature selection is for model efficiency, not output reduction. Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with module enrichment. Define a procedure to derive co-expression modules (e.g., hierarchical clustering or WGCNA on control cells) and use them for enrichment of predicted top genes. Include a step to map predicted top genes to modules and report module-level enrichment scores (e.g., hypergeometric test) for each perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that selected features capture known perturbation effects (e.g., knockdown of target gene itself); verify pathway annotations cover substantial fraction of genes; for interpretability, check enrichment of predicted top genes for modules related to the target gene. Check that derived co-expression modules are biologically coherent and stable across control cell subsamples.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend retaining all genes in the output and using HVGs or all genes for model training depending on method. External annotations allowed only if task permits; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"types\\\": [\\n            \\\"Gene identifiers (same order as input)\\\",\\n            \\\"Normalization factors (counts per 10k)\\\",\\n            \\\"sgRNA assignments\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Gene annotation quality assumed consistent; normalization already applied. Verify gene identifiers are consistent and match evaluator expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"selection\\\": [\\n            \\\"Filter genes with zero expression across all cells (if any) for modeling, but output must include all genes; handle zero-variance genes by excluding from model input but retaining in output and predicting control mean\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical features should not drive biological predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Verify gene order and normalization match the evaluator's expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_technical\\\": \\\"No decision support. Ensure predictions.npz has perts in the exact order of heldout_perturbations.json and means with shape (n_perts, n_genes), float dtype.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"Number of cells per perturbation\\\",\\n            \\\"Knockdown efficiency: 1 - (mean target gene expression in perturbation cells / mean target gene expression in control cells), computed in log1p space\\\",\\n            \\\"Reproducibility of perturbation effects across cells: within-perturbation variance vs. between-perturbation variance\\\",\\n            \\\"Baseline expression of target genes\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Quality varies by perturbation; some may have few cells and noisy means. Compute and report per-perturbation cell counts, target gene expression reduction, and within-perturbation variance. Prioritize quality issues by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization: low cell numbers and batch confounding are most critical; knockdown efficiency and doublets are next.\\\",\\n          \\\"issues\\\": [\\n            \\\"Low cell numbers for some perturbations\\\",\\n            \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n            \\\"Off-target effects\\\",\\n            \\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Weighted mean estimation by cell quality\\\",\\n            \\\"Shrinkage of perturbation means toward control for low-cell perturbations with cross-validated shrinkage parameter: blend predicted effect with control mean using weight w tuned by cross-validation over perturbations (e.g., grid search w in [0,1])\\\",\\n            \\\"Define minimum cell threshold (e.g., 20 cells) for including perturbations in training; for perturbations with <20 cells, consider excluding from training or using strong shrinkage (w > 0.9)\\\",\\n            \\\"For perturbations targeting unexpressed genes (control mean < 1 CP10K), predict control mean with strong shrinkage (weight > 0.9) and flag in validation\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare known essential gene knockdowns to expected phenotypes. Check stability of perturbation means under cell subsampling (correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9 for perturbations with >20 cells). Compute per-perturbation cell counts, target gene knockdown, and within-perturbation variance; report these as part of quality assessment. Validate knockdown efficiency for a set of known essential genes (e.g., ribosomal genes) and confirm expected reduction (e.g., >50% for strong knockdowns).\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend computing per-perturbation cell counts and target gene knockdown to assess training data quality. Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells to detect imbalances. Implement a quality assessment module that outputs a table of metrics per perturbation and flags low-quality perturbations. Use this table to decide on inclusion/exclusion of perturbations in training and to inform shrinkage strategies.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"technical\\\": {\\n          \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"n_genes per cell\\\",\\n            \\\"total counts per cell\\\",\\n            \\\"percent mitochondrial reads (if raw counts available)\\\",\\n            \\\"doublet scores (if available)\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Standard scRNA-seq QC metrics should be computed from the h5ad. Inspect layers/obsm for raw counts and QC metrics; if absent, compute what is possible from X and document limitations.\\\",\\n          \\\"issues\\\": [\\n            \\\"Low-quality cells\\\",\\n            \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n            \\\"Batch effects\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n            \\\"Filter cells with low n_genes or high pct_mito using data-driven thresholds (e.g., median \\u00b1 3 MAD); if computable, also consider explicit thresholds like n_genes < 200 or pct_mito > 20%, justified by data distribution\\\",\\n            \\\"Remove doublets if scores available; otherwise consider a simple doublet detection method on highly variable genes or flag as a limitation\\\",\\n            \\\"Include batch as covariate if confounded (Cram\\u00e9r's V > 0.3); avoid aggressive batch correction\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls (minimum 10 cells retained per perturbation); compare perturbation means before and after QC filtering; ensure key markers (e.g., GATA1, HBB in K562) and control baseline are stable. Validate that quality filtering improves cross-validation performance; compare models with and without QC. Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells (e.g., Mann-Whitney U test) to detect imbalances.\\\",\\n          \\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support. Recommend performing QC before computing perturbation means for training. Document all QC parameters and filtered cell/gene lists. Run cross-validation with different QC thresholds and select the threshold that maximizes validation performance without removing entire perturbations. Create a QC report with per-perturbation cell counts and flag low-quality perturbations.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"noise\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": {\\n          \\\"sources\\\": [\\n            \\\"Cell cycle heterogeneity\\\",\\n            \\\"Clonal variation\\\",\\n            \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n            \\\"Stochastic gene expression\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Increases variance of perturbation means, especially for low-cell perturbations.\\\",\\n         \",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": {\\n    \\\"problem_definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"formal_definition\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_context\\\": \\\"We predict the transcriptome-wide mean expression response of K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells to CRISPRi knockdown of individual genes (perturbations) not observed during training. Training data are single-cell RNA-seq profiles from K562 cells under many CRISPRi perturbations and controls. Held-out perturbations target unseen genes; the model must generalize from gene-level features and perturbation structure to unseen knockdowns, producing mean log1p CP10K expression for all genes in the same order and normalization as the input. Mandatory metadata verification: before any modeling, inspect h5ad obs to confirm cell_line == 'K562', perturbation_type == 'CRISPRi', control label column and values (e.g., 'control' or 'non-targeting'), batch/plate columns, and gene identifiers; document actual values and flag any deviation from assumptions. All assumptions about cell line, perturbation type, and control labels must be verified directly from h5ad metadata before modeling.\\\",\\n        \\\"input_output_mapping\\\": \\\"Input: training scRNA-seq matrix X (cells x genes) with perturbation labels and control cells; held-out perturbation list P_held (n_perts); gene-level metadata (gene identifiers, optionally embeddings/annotations derivable from provided data). Output: means matrix M (n_perts x n_genes) where M[i,j] is the predicted mean log1p CP10K expression of gene j in K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbation P_held[i]. Evaluation compares M to held-out true perturbation means using pearson_delta (Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control per perturbation, computed in log1p CP10K space as predicted_mean - control_mean), overall MSE, and top-20 DE gene MSE/PCC/R2. Top-20 DE genes are defined as those with largest absolute true delta from control in held-out data, as per the evaluator. Delta computation clarified: delta = log1p(mean_pert) - log1p(mean_control), i.e., difference of log1p-transformed means, consistent with evaluator.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_constraints\\\": [\\n          \\\"Predictions must be non-negative after inverse log1p transform and consistent with CP10K normalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Predicted means should reflect biologically plausible expression ranges for K562 genes; define plausible range operationally as the 1st-99th percentile of control cell expression per gene (in log1p CP10K space), and flag predictions outside this range for review.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects should be sparse and concentrated on biologically related genes/pathways.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control baseline must be estimated from training control cells, not assumed zero; verify control labels and representativeness across batches (Pearson correlation of control means across batches > 0.9).\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are gene-specific and often weak, requiring shrinkage toward control; shrinkage parameter should be tuned by cross-validation over perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no direct training examples; model must rely on gene-gene relationships and functional similarity derivable from provided data.\\\",\\n          \\\"No external data from the same screen may be used; only cellforge_input.h5ad. External pathway annotations may be used only if explicitly permitted; otherwise derive gene features from expression data alone.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562 should be predicted near control mean with strong shrinkage; define rule: if control mean expression < 1 CP10K, predict control mean with shrinkage weight > 0.9, and flag such cases in validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Zero-variance genes (no expression variation across cells) should be predicted as control mean and flagged in output metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"Off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations and must be treated as a limitation; assess off-target effects for training perturbations by checking correlation of effect vectors between perturbations targeting similar genes/pathways.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562-specific copy-number alterations (e.g., amplification of BCR-ABL1 region) may confound expression; assess by checking expression of genes in commonly amplified regions and flag as potential confounders.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"evaluation_criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control per perturbation (pearson_delta), where delta = log1p(mean_pert) - log1p(mean_control) in log1p CP10K space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and true means across all genes and perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 restricted to the top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation, defined from true held-out data.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological plausibility: predicted effects should align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi knockdown direction; validate using pre-specified gene sets (e.g., ribosomal protein genes RPS/RPL, proteasome subunits PSMA/PSMB) and expected direction of change.\\\",\\n          \\\"Reproducibility: predictions must be generated from a deterministic or seeded pipeline using only provided data, with fixed seeds, pinned software versions, saved cross-validation folds, and archived intermediate artifacts.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"success_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta above a baseline of zero-effect (control-only) prediction and above simple linear baselines; target pearson_delta > 0.3 if achievable, to be confirmed by pilot experiments.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE lower than control-only baseline and lower than naive gene-mean baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene PCC and R2 significantly above zero-effect baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Consistent improvement across perturbations rather than driven by a few outliers.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological validation: predicted top genes for known pathways show expected knockdown signatures where prior knowledge exists (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown downregulates translation machinery). Select at least 5 known essential genes in K562 (e.g., RPS3, RPL13, PSMA1, RPL5, RPS6) and check that their knockdown reduces expression of translation/proteasome machinery with expected direction.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"key_variables\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": [\\n          \\\"Perturbation target gene identity and its functional role in K562.\\\",\\n          \\\"Baseline expression of target gene and pathway members; flag targets with zero/near-zero baseline expression (control mean < 1 CP10K).\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships and co-expression modules derived from provided data.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown efficiency and off-target effects (quantify for training perturbations; treat as unobserved for held-out).\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell cycle, stress, and culture-state variation in K562 (assess via QC metrics and cell cycle scores if available).\\\",\\n          \\\"Copy-number alterations and karyotype of K562 affecting expression (acknowledge as confounder; assess via expression of genes in commonly amplified regions).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical\\\": [\\n          \\\"Single-cell library size and dropout.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects across perturbation plates or runs; test association with perturbation labels (chi-square test, Cram\\u00e9r's V).\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide RNA representation and multiplicity of infection.\\\",\\n          \\\"Normalization (CP10K, log1p) and gene filtering; verify normalization is already applied in h5ad by checking value ranges and comparing to raw counts if available.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbation list and gene ordering; ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in output and feature space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Train/test split by perturbation (unseen-perturbation setting).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relationships\\\": [\\n          \\\"Perturbation target gene expression is reduced in its own knockdown cells (on-target effect); quantify for training perturbations as knockdown efficiency = 1 - (mean target gene expression in perturbation cells / mean target gene expression in control cells) in log1p space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Knockdown of a regulator propagates to downstream targets and pathways.\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes with similar functions or co-expression tend to have correlated perturbation responses.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean expression is the baseline from which deltas are computed.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may share pathway membership with training perturbations, enabling transfer; quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations (percentage of held-out targets with pathway overlap with training).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"constraints\\\": [\\n          \\\"Only cellforge_input.h5ad may be used for training.\\\",\\n          \\\"No held-out perturbation cells are available for training or fine-tuning.\\\",\\n          \\\"Output must match gene order and normalization exactly; implement automated format checks (keys 'perts' and 'means', shapes, dtype float).\\\",\\n          \\\"Predictions are means, not single-cell distributions; bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational resources should be reasonable for a single-machine pipeline (at least 32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_requirements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Hold out a subset of training perturbations to simulate unseen-perturbation prediction; define folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare against control-only and gene-mean baselines.\\\",\\n          \\\"Report pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics on internal validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Check biological coherence of predicted top genes for validation perturbations; compare to known essential gene knockdowns (e.g., ribosomal genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure deterministic seeds and documented preprocessing; re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions (max absolute difference < 1e-6); compare outputs across seeds (Pearson correlation > 0.95).\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps (HVG selection, scaling) are fit on training data only to avoid leakage; save fitted transformers.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate that QC filtering does not remove entire perturbations or controls (minimum 10 cells retained per perturbation); compare perturbation means before and after QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"Metadata verification: inspect h5ad obs for cell_line, perturbation, control, batch, and QC columns; document actual values and confirm assumptions (K562, CRISPRi, control labels).\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify pathway/co-expression module coverage of training perturbations to estimate transferability to held-out targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define and validate rule for perturbations targeting unexpressed genes: predict control mean with strong shrinkage (weight > 0.9) and flag in validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate that predicted means fall within plausible ranges (1st-99th percentile of control expression) for most genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perform subsampling stability analysis: randomly split cells per perturbation into two halves, compute means, require Pearson correlation > 0.9 for perturbations with >20 cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare QC metrics between control and perturbed cells (Mann-Whitney U test, p < 0.05) to detect imbalances.\\\",\\n          \\\"Test association between batch and perturbation labels (chi-square test) and report Cram\\u00e9r's V; if V > 0.3, include batch as covariate.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_scope\\\": \\\"K562 cells under CRISPRi perturbations; prediction of mean transcriptional response to unseen gene knockdowns; focus on gene-level mean expression and delta from control. Scope assumes control cells are representative for held-out perturbations; this must be checked across batches (Pearson correlation of control means across batches > 0.9). Perturbations targeting unexpressed genes (control mean < 1 CP10K) should be predicted near control mean with strong shrinkage (weight > 0.9) and flagged. Bimodal responses are acknowledged as a limitation of mean-based evaluation. K562-specific copy-number effects should be assessed by checking expression of genes in commonly amplified regions.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_scope\\\": \\\"Single-cell RNA-seq matrix processing, control baseline estimation, feature construction from gene identity and expression, regression/transfer learning from training perturbations to held-out perturbations, and evaluation with Gene-Mila metrics. Feature representation questions focus on features derivable from provided data (co-expression, target expression) unless external annotations are permitted. Preprocessing (HVG selection, scaling) must be fit on training data only and applied to held-out perturbations; save fitted transformers for reproducibility.\\\",\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cannot model single-cell heterogeneity or cell-state-specific responses.\\\",\\n          \\\"Limited ability to predict strong effects for perturbations with no pathway overlap with training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi efficiency and off-target effects are not directly observed for held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562-specific copy-number and culture effects may confound generalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation is on means, so sparse or bimodal responses may be poorly captured.\\\",\\n          \\\"Assumption that perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function may not hold for all genes (e.g., off-target effects).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control cells in training are representative of the control baseline for held-out perturbations; verify by comparing control means across batches (Pearson correlation > 0.9).\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene identifiers are consistent and sufficient to derive functional features; verify coverage of pathway annotations if used.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects are largely mediated by target gene function and pathway membership.\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean expression is a meaningful summary of perturbation response.\\\",\\n          \\\"Training perturbations cover enough functional diversity to allow transfer to held-out perturbations; quantify pathway and essential gene coverage.\\\",\\n          \\\"If external pathway annotations are used, they are permitted by task rules; otherwise derive features from expression data alone.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch is not confounded with perturbation labels; if Cram\\u00e9r's V > 0.3, include batch as covariate.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use internal held-out perturbations to verify that predicted deltas recover known pathway signatures, that on-target knockdown is reflected for target genes when possible, and that top predicted genes are enriched for relevant biological processes. Select at least 5 known essential genes in K562 (e.g., RPS3, RPL13, PSMA1, RPL5, RPS6) and check that their knockdown produces expected expression changes (e.g., reduction in translation/proteasome machinery). Use literature-based signatures to validate predicted top genes for these perturbations. Confirm predicted effects for selected perturbations with independent qPCR or targeted RNA-seq where feasible. Check that predicted effects for zero-baseline targets are near control mean and flag them.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"key_challenges\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"Unseen perturbations may target genes with no close functional analog in training.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown effects are often weak and variable, making deltas hard to predict.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562 has complex copy-number alterations that distort expression and perturbation response.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway redundancy and feedback can mask or reverse expected effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects may depend on cell state not captured in mean expression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations targeting genes with zero or near-zero baseline expression in K562.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Poor generalization to functionally isolated perturbations and underestimation of true effects, reducing pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics. The most critical challenges are distribution shift to unseen perturbations and noisy means; off-target effects and copy-number confounders are next in priority.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use gene functional embeddings and pathway annotations to relate held-out targets to training perturbations only if permitted; otherwise derive co-expression modules from provided data (e.g., PCA or hierarchical clustering on control cells, select modules with >10 genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrink predictions toward control to avoid spurious large effects; operationalize shrinkage by blending predicted effect with control mean using weight w tuned by cross-validation over perturbations (grid search w in [0,1]).\\\",\\n          \\\"Model perturbation effects as low-rank or pathway-level modules rather than independent gene effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Incorporate target gene baseline expression and network centrality as features; predict near control mean with strong shrinkage (w > 0.9) for targets with zero baseline expression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust control baseline estimation across batches; prefer including batch as covariate over batch correction when confounding is possible (Cram\\u00e9r's V > 0.3).\\\",\\n          \\\"For perturbations with <20 cells, consider excluding from training or using strong shrinkage (w > 0.9).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Internal leave-perturbation-out validation stratified by pathway and target expression to assess generalization across functional categories. Use pilot experiments to confirm which challenges actually degrade performance. Validate mitigation strategies via cross-validation: compare performance with and without shrinkage, with and without module-based features. Check that shrinkage does not eliminate known strong effects (e.g., ribosomal gene knockdown).\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_considerations\\\": \\\"Predictions should respect directionality of known regulatory relationships and avoid biologically implausible large effects for genes not connected to the target. Check that mitigations preserve known biological effects. Assess whether predicted effects for perturbations with no pathway overlap are near control mean, as expected.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"High-dimensional output (thousands of genes) with limited number of perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sparse single-cell data and dropout affecting mean estimates.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects between perturbation groups and controls; test association with perturbation labels (chi-square test, Cram\\u00e9r's V).\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision.\\\",\\n          \\\"Need to match exact gene order and normalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential non-determinism in model training (e.g., neural networks).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Overfitting to training perturbations, noisy control baseline, and poor calibration of predicted means.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Aggregate to pseudo-bulk means per perturbation to reduce noise.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use low-rank regression, factor models, or kernel methods on gene features.\\\",\\n          \\\"Apply strong regularization and cross-validation over perturbations; define and save folds by perturbation ID.\\\",\\n          \\\"Estimate control mean robustly using all control cells and batch covariates; compare control mean estimates across batches.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate output shape, gene order, and normalization against input; implement automated format checks.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps, record software versions, use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with 'svd' solver), and save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Cross-validation by perturbation with held-out metric computation and sanity checks on output dimensions and value ranges. Re-run pipeline with same seed and confirm identical predictions (max absolute difference < 1e-6); compare outputs across seeds to assess stability (Pearson correlation > 0.95).\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation_requirements\\\": \\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and optionally torch; deterministic seeds; pinned software versions (requirements.txt); memory sufficient for the full matrix and model (at least 32 GB RAM). Implement preprocessing as a scikit-learn Pipeline or custom transformer fit on training data and applied to held-out perturbations; save fitted transformer.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"data_quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Variable cell numbers per perturbation; quantify and flag low-cell perturbations (<20 cells).\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-expression genes with high dropout.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential guide-level or batch confounding; test association between batch and perturbation labels (chi-square test, Cram\\u00e9r's V).\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cells may differ in quality from perturbed cells; compare QC metrics (Mann-Whitney U test).\\\",\\n          \\\"Possible doublets or low-quality cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unclear whether raw counts are available; if only normalized X is present, mitochondrial fraction and doublet scores may not be computable.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Noisy perturbation means and biased control baseline, leading to inaccurate delta predictions. Quality issues should be prioritized by expected impact on mean estimation and generalization\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"baseline_models\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"literature_review\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"existing_methods\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"methods\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean baseline (predict control mean for all perturbations)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear baseline (Ridge regression from perturbation one-hot or learned embeddings to mean expression)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen perturbations, e.g., Sci-Plex-style linear effect transfer)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Latent linear models (PCA on control cells + regression, e.g., scGen-style linear approximation)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Autoencoder-based perturbation prediction (e.g., scGen, CPA)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph neural network models leveraging gene regulatory networks (e.g., GEARS)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Foundation-model zero-shot baselines (e.g., scGPT, Geneformer fine-tuned or prompted)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor / similarity-based perturbation transfer (e.g., using perturbation embeddings derived from target gene features)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"strengths\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean baseline provides a lower bound and reveals how much signal is above biological/technical noise.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear baselines are highly interpretable, fast, robust in low-data regimes, and often competitive for mean-expression prediction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Effect-size transfer models are simple, biologically motivated (additive shifts in log-normalized space), and can generalize to similar perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Autoencoder and latent-variable models can capture nonlinear expression manifolds and disentangle perturbation effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph-based models (GEARS) incorporate biological prior knowledge (gene-gene relationships) and can generalize to unseen perturbations via pathway similarity.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Foundation models leverage large-scale pretraining and may capture general gene-regulatory patterns, enabling zero-shot or few-shot prediction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Hybrid and multi-task approaches can combine interpretability of linear models with nonlinear flexibility and share statistical strength across perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean baseline ignores perturbation-specific effects entirely and is only useful as a sanity check.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects; they may underfit complex regulatory responses.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Effect-size transfer assumes additive effects and may fail when perturbations interact or when target genes have context-dependent functions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Autoencoder-based methods often require large training sets of perturbations and may overfit to seen perturbations; generalization to unseen perturbations is challenging.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph neural networks depend heavily on the quality and completeness of prior networks; K562-specific regulatory networks may be incomplete, and external network data may not be allowed under provided-data-only constraints.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Foundation models are computationally expensive, may have data leakage concerns, and often require fine-tuning to achieve competitive performance on specific screens; their zero-shot performance on K562 CRISPRi is uncertain.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Most methods predict single-cell distributions or pseudobulk, but the task requires mean expression; model outputs may need careful aggregation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Evaluation metrics (e.g., Pearson delta) can be dominated by highly expressed genes, and top-20 DE gene metrics are sensitive to noise.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Performance on perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression in K562 is unclear; models may need explicit shrinkage toward control.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_relevance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Perturbation prediction models should capture biologically meaningful gene programs (e.g., CRISPRi knockdown effects on essential genes, pathway responses). Simple linear/additive models reflect the assumption that perturbation effects are approximately additive in log-normalized space, a reasonable first-order approximation. Graph-based and foundation models aim to encode regulatory relationships, but their biological interpretability is often limited. For K562 CRISPRi, the most relevant signals are likely direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses; models that explicitly model gene-gene interactions or use pathway priors may be more biologically grounded. However, biological relevance must be verified against actual h5ad metadata: confirm cell line (K562), perturbation type (CRISPRi), control labels, and that training perturbations cover diverse pathways and essential genes. Compute target gene knockdown efficiency per training perturbation and flag weak or absent on-target effects. Assess confounding with cell cycle, culture state, or copy-number alterations. Map gene identifiers to pathway and network databases (e.g., MSigDB, STRING) only if external annotations are permitted; otherwise derive gene features from provided expression data (e.g., co-expression, target expression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"The h5ad file contains raw counts or normalized expression in X, and metadata in obs (perturbation labels, control labels, batch info).\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cell line is K562 and perturbation type is CRISPRi, as stated in task description; this will be verified from metadata.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"External annotations (pathway databases, gene networks) are not allowed; all features must be derived from provided data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"The evaluator computes delta as log1p(mean_pert) - log1p(mean_control) in CP10K space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Compute resources are limited to a single GPU (e.g., 16GB memory) and reasonable CPU time; foundation models may not be feasible.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"model_comparison\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"comparison_criteria\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Predictive accuracy on unseen perturbations (Pearson delta, MSE, top-20 DE metrics)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Generalization ability to held-out perturbations and cell types; quantify pathway coverage of training perturbations to estimate feasibility\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Biological interpretability of learned representations and predictions; ability to recover known K562 CRISPRi signatures\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Computational efficiency (training time, inference time, memory); verify available compute and plan efficient implementations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Data efficiency (performance as a function of training set size); account for low-cell perturbations via shrinkage or exclusion\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Robustness to biological and technical noise (batch effects, stochasticity); test association between batch and perturbation labels\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ease of implementation and reproducibility; specify seeds, software versions, and saved CV folds\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Handling of perturbations targeting genes with zero baseline expression (e.g., shrinkage toward control)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits (e.g., Pearson correlation > 0.95)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"performance_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control (delta computed in log1p space as predicted mean minus control mean)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Mean squared error (MSE) on mean expression across all genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE, Pearson correlation, and R2 on top-20 differentially expressed genes (defined as genes with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Area under the precision-recall curve for identifying differentially expressed genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cosine similarity between predicted and true perturbation effect vectors\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Mean absolute error (MAE)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes (e.g., slope of true vs predicted delta)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_interpretability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Linear models offer direct interpretability via coefficients that can be mapped to gene effects. Graph-based models can highlight pathways and gene modules. Foundation models are generally black-box, though attention weights may provide some insight. For biological validation, predicted top DE genes should be compared to known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes leads to growth arrest signatures; ribosomal gene knockdown should downregulate translation machinery). Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space, even if not expressed in training, to allow on-target effect interpretation. Report predicted deltas for top genes per perturbation with pathway enrichment (if external annotations allowed) or co-expression module enrichment (if not).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"computational_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Linear baselines require minimal compute (seconds to minutes on CPU). Autoencoder and graph models require GPUs and hours of training. Foundation models require significant GPU memory and may need fine-tuning, which can take days. For this task, given the dataset size and evaluation constraints, simple baselines are likely sufficient and more practical. Verify available compute and memory; plan for efficient implementations. Use deterministic solvers where possible (e.g., Ridge with 'svd' solver) and document any non-deterministic components.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"The dataset size is moderate (e.g., <100 perturbations, <100k cells), making simple models feasible.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"GPU resources may be available but are not guaranteed; prioritize CPU-friendly methods.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"External networks are not allowed; graph-based models must use co-expression networks derived from control cells.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"recommendations\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"model_selection\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Start with strong simple baselines: control-mean, linear regression (Ridge) from perturbation embeddings (e.g., one-hot or learned embeddings) to mean expression, and effect-size transfer.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Implement a latent linear model (PCA on control cells + regression) to capture major expression axes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"If resources permit, evaluate a graph-based model (GEARS) or a lightweight autoencoder (scGen) for comparison; use external networks only if allowed; otherwise use co-expression networks from control cells.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Avoid heavy foundation models unless zero-shot performance is competitive and computational budget allows.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Consider hybrid models combining linear effects with nonlinear residual corrections and multi-task learning across perturbations to share statistical strength.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Establish a minimum acceptable performance (e.g., pearson_delta > 0) and a fallback to control-mean with shrinkage if simple models fail to beat control baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Include a shrinkage estimator blending control mean with effect-size transfer, with shrinkage weight tuned by cross-validation over perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"implementation_priority\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"First, establish control-mean and linear regression baselines to set a performance floor; run pilot experiments early to assess feasibility.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Second, implement effect-size transfer and PCA-based regression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Third, if time permits, implement a simple neural network (MLP) on perturbation embeddings or a hybrid model.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Focus on robust cross-validation and evaluation using the specified metrics; define CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression (at least 5 folds if enough perturbations).\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Implement automated format checks: load predictions.npz, assert keys 'perts' and 'means', check shapes and order against heldout_perturbations.json, verify dtype is float.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ensure all preprocessing steps (e.g., HVG selection, scaling) are fit on training data only to avoid leakage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Maintain a decision log documenting assumptions, verification steps, and rationale for model choices.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Validate predictions by checking whether known K562 CRISPRi effects are recovered. Select at least 5 known essential genes in K562 (e.g., RPS3, RPL13, PSMA1, RPL5, RPS6) and check that their knockdown reduces expression of translation/proteasome machinery. Compare predicted top DE genes with known pathways using gene set enrichment analysis on predicted effect vectors if external annotations are allowed; otherwise use co-expression modules derived from control cells. Define quantitative thresholds for mean stability (e.g., correlation of means across subsamples > 0.9) and validate that QC filtering does not remove cells with strong perturbation effects disproportionately. Assess copy-number effects by checking expression of genes in commonly amplified regions in K562 (e.g., MYC on chr8) and flag potential confounders.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"evaluation_framework\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"primary_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change from control (delta computed in log1p space)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE on mean expression across all genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE on top-20 DE genes (per perturbation, averaged; DE genes defined as those with largest absolute delta in true held-out data)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"secondary_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson correlation on top-20 DE genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Spearman correlation of gene rankings by effect size\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cosine similarity between predicted and true delta vectors\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Mean absolute error (MAE)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes (e.g., slope of true vs predicted delta)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Recovery of known pathway signatures (e.g., GSEA on predicted deltas, if external annotations allowed; otherwise co-expression module enrichment)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Precision-recall for identifying true DE genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Correlation with known essential gene knockdown signatures (e.g., ribosomal genes in K562)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Conservation of gene-gene co-expression patterns in predicted profiles\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cross-validation performance across held-out perturbations (leave-one-perturbation-out; at least 5 folds if enough perturbations)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stability of predictions across random seeds and data splits (e.g., Pearson correlation > 0.95)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Robustness to noise injection (e.g., dropout, batch effects)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Statistical significance testing against baselines (e.g., permutation test)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_strategy\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"cross_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation on the training set to estimate generalization to unseen perturbations. Define and save CV folds by perturbation ID with stratification by pathway or target expression to ensure representative splits; use at least 5 folds if enough perturbations. Given the held-out set is fixed, simulate the unseen-perturbation setting by holding out perturbations from training. Compare model performance with and without QC and batch covariates to justify preprocessing choices. Use nested cross-validation for unbiased performance estimation. Save fold assignments and random seeds for reproducibility.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Assess biological plausibility of predictions via pathway enrichment (if external annotations allowed) or co-expression module enrichment (if not) and comparison to known K562 CRISPRi phenotypes. Use independent data (if available) or literature-based signatures to validate top predicted effects. Select known essential genes in K562 (e.g., RPS3, RPL13, PSMA1) and check expected expression changes. Ensure target genes of held-out perturbations are retained in the output and model feature space. For a subset of training perturbations with known biology, verify that predicted top genes are enriched in modules related to the target gene's function (e.g., ribosomal module for ribosomal gene knockdown).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"independent_testing\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The provided held-out perturbations serve as an independent test set. Ensure no data leakage: models must not see any cells from held-out perturbations during training. Use only the provided input file for training. Verify that held-out perturbations are truly absent from training. Run a dummy submission with control mean to confirm evaluator accepts format.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"robustness_testing\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Evaluate model performance under different normalization schemes, random gene subsampling, and noise addition. Test sensitivity to hyperparameters and random initialization. Use fixed random seeds for all stochastic steps and record software versions. Re-run the pipeline with the same seed and confirm identical predictions (max absolute difference < 1e-6); compare outputs across seeds to assess stability (Pearson correlation > 0.95). Save intermediate artifacts (QC-filtered data, control mean, perturbation means, model coefficients) in a versioned directory with a manifest file.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"benchmarks\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"baseline_models\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean predictor (predicts control mean for all perturbations)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear regression (Ridge) with one-hot perturbation encoding\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Effect-size transfer (average effect of seen perturbations applied to unseen)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"PCA-based linear model (PCA on control, regression on perturbation)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Shrinkage estimator blending control mean with effect-size transfer, with cross-validated shrinkage parameter\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"state_of_the_art\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"GEARS (graph-enhanced perturbation prediction; use external networks only if allowed; otherwise co-expression networks)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"scGen (autoencoder-based)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"CPA (compositional perturbation autoencoder)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) if applicable and computationally feasible\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_benchmarks\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Known K562 CRISPRi effects from literature (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, ribosomal genes)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pathway-level concordance with known perturbation signatures (if external annotations allowed; otherwise co-expression modules)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Gene-gene correlation structure preservation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"On-target knockdown efficiency per training perturbation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"performance_targets\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson delta > 0.3 (indicative of meaningful signal)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE MSE lower than control-mean baseline\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"R2 on top-20 DE genes > 0.1\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Performance significantly better than control-mean baseline (p < 0.05 via permutation test)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Minimum acceptable performance: pearson_delta > 0; fallback to control-mean with shrinkage if not met\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"performance_analysis\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"model_performance\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"accuracy_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson delta per perturbation (mean and distribution)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE across all genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE MSE, Pearson, R2\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Spearman correlation of gene effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stability across subsamples (e.g., correlation of means > 0.9)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_relevance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Models that capture direct knockdown effects and downstream pathway responses will show higher Pearson delta. Simple linear models may perform wel